2026-09-04 04:26:40 [gemini-3.8-flash] No, these are not development modules—they are the actual low-level hardware drivers required for
2026-09-04 04:26:40 [gemini-3.8-flash] your Intensity Pro 4K to function.
2026-09-04 04:26:40 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-04 04:26:40 [gemini-3.8-flash] In this context, DKMS stands for Dynamic Kernel Module Support.
2026-09-04 04:26:40 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-04 04:26:40 [gemini-3.8-flash] Because Blackmagic's drivers are proprietary and not built directly into the official Linux kernel
2026-09-04 04:26:40 [gemini-3.8-flash] source tree, DKMS compiles the driver modules (`blackmagic` and `blackmagic-io`) specifically for
2026-09-04 04:26:40 [gemini-3.8-flash] your running kernel (`7.2.2-arch1-1`). This mechanism also ensures that whenever your kernel
2026-09-04 04:26:40 [gemini-3.8-flash] updates in the future, the system will automatically recompile these hardware drivers so the card
2026-09-04 04:26:40 [gemini-3.8-flash] continues to work.
2026-09-04 04:26:40 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-04 04:26:40 [gemini-3.8-flash] Now that the kernel drivers are built and installed, you can proceed with starting the background
2026-09-04 04:26:40 [gemini-3.8-flash] helper service:
