2026-09-04 04:38:24 [skid@archlinux] is it not due to include path?
2026-09-04 04:38:30 [gemini-3.8-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 gemini-3.8-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.007 27.65KB 6.69s
2026-09-04 04:38:30 [gemini-3.8-flash] It is related, but with an important distinction:
2026-09-04 04:38:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-04 04:38:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] `DeckLinkAPIDispatch.cpp` is a C++ source file, not a header (`.h`) file.
2026-09-04 04:38:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-04 04:38:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] Standard compiler include paths (such as `-I/usr/include`) tell the compiler where to find
2026-09-04 04:38:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] `#include` headers during compilation. However, `make` is trying to compile
2026-09-04 04:38:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] `DeckLinkAPIDispatch.cpp` itself directly into an object file, and it is looking for that file at
2026-09-04 04:38:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] the hardcoded prerequisite path:
2026-09-04 04:38:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-04 04:38:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-04 04:38:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] ../../include/DeckLinkAPIDispatch.cpp
2026-09-04 04:38:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-04 04:38:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-04 04:38:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] Because that relative directory does not exist relative to where you cloned `bmdtools`, `make`
2026-09-04 04:38:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] aborts before the compiler is even invoked.
2026-09-04 04:38:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
