2026-09-05 07:21:17 [skid@archlinux] another idea is an edit command with a builtin text editor
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 gemini-3.8-flash 🌟 🪣 0.7° $0.047 175.8KB 6.71s
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] A built-in console editor inside the relay environment is an interesting addition, especially since
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] `slopprompt.ts` already implements raw terminal I/O.
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] When considering a built-in editor versus invoking an external editor (`$EDITOR`), there are a
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] couple of architectural directions to evaluate:
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 1. Minimal Line / Chunk Editor (ed / ex style)
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] • How it works: Works within standard scrolling terminal output without needing full-screen
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] alternate buffers. Commands allow viewing a window of lines, replacing lines by index, inserting
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] text, appending, or regex replacement.
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Advantages:
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash]   * Simple state machine.
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash]   * Preserves conversation/terminal scrollback cleanly.
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash]   * Fits neatly into existing line-based input routines without complex terminal redraw loops.
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash]   * Very easy to script or automate via LLM tool calls.
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 2. Full-Screen Visual Editor (nano / kilo style)
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] • How it works: Switches terminal to the alternate screen buffer (`\x1b[?1049h`), captures
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] raw keystrokes (navigation arrows, backspace, page up/down), renders a status bar, and redraws rows
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] on change. On exit (`Ctrl+Q` or `Ctrl+X`), switches back to the primary screen (`\x1b[?1049l`).
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Core Components:
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash]   * Buffer State: An array of lines (`string[]`) or a gap buffer, cursor position
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] (`cursorRow`, `cursorCol`), scroll offsets (`rowOffset`, `colOffset`), and a dirty flag.
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash]   * Input Dispatcher: A named key-handling loop decoding escape sequences (cursor movement,
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] backspace, Enter, delete, page up/down, key combos).
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash]   * Screen Renderer: Clears screen, positions cursor via ANSI sequences (`\x1b[H`,
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] `\x1b[<r>;<c>H`), renders text rows clipped to `Deno.consoleSize()`, and paints a footer/status bar
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] showing filename, modified state, and shortcuts.
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash]   * Lifecycle & Cleanup: Rigorous signal handling (`SIGINT`, `SIGTERM`) and `try...finally`
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] blocks to guarantee the terminal exits raw mode and restores the primary screen buffer if anything
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] throws.
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 3. Subprocess Delegation (`$EDITOR` fallback)
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Temporarily pause raw mode, spawn the user's preferred editor (`$EDITOR`, `nano`, `vim`, or
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] `micro`) with `stdin`, `stdout`, and `stderr` set to `"inherit"`, await process completion, and
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] then resume `relay`'s prompt loop.
2026-09-05 07:21:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
