2026-09-05 07:23:18 [gemini-3.8-flash] There is currently no official or widely adopted "nano" clone package in the Deno standard library
2026-09-05 07:23:18 [gemini-3.8-flash] or JSR ecosystem.
2026-09-05 07:23:18 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:23:18 [gemini-3.8-flash] A few community experiments and ports exist—primarily TypeScript ports of Salvatore Sanfilippo’s
2026-09-05 07:23:18 [gemini-3.8-flash] (antirez) Kilo editor, which is the classic ~1000-line C baseline that nano-style minimal
2026-09-05 07:23:18 [gemini-3.8-flash] editors are built on. However, none have achieved the status of an established, maintained utility
2026-09-05 07:23:18 [gemini-3.8-flash] library in Deno.
2026-09-05 07:23:18 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:23:18 [gemini-3.8-flash] Most CLI tools in the Deno and Node ecosystems handle editing by either:
2026-09-05 07:23:18 [gemini-3.8-flash] 1. Delegating to `$EDITOR`: Spawning `nano`, `micro`, or `vim` via `Deno.Command` with
2026-09-05 07:23:18 [gemini-3.8-flash] inherited stdio.
2026-09-05 07:23:18 [gemini-3.8-flash] 2. Building a custom lightweight visual editor: Since `slopprompt.ts` already sets up raw
2026-09-05 07:23:18 [gemini-3.8-flash] mode and basic key reading, implementing a self-contained Kilo/nano clone in pure TypeScript takes
2026-09-05 07:23:18 [gemini-3.8-flash] roughly 300–400 lines.
2026-09-05 07:23:18 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:23:18 [gemini-3.8-flash] The architecture for a native Deno nano-style editor typically consists of:
2026-09-05 07:23:18 [gemini-3.8-flash] • An alternate screen buffer context (`\x1b[?1049h` on enter, `\x1b[?1049l` on exit).
2026-09-05 07:23:18 [gemini-3.8-flash] • A line buffer (`string[]`) with cursor tracking (`cx`, `cy`) and viewport offsets (`rowOffset`,
2026-09-05 07:23:18 [gemini-3.8-flash] `colOffset`).
2026-09-05 07:23:18 [gemini-3.8-flash] • A unified keyboard reader parsing arrow keys, backspace, page navigation, and control chords
2026-09-05 07:23:18 [gemini-3.8-flash] (such as `Ctrl+O` for write-out and `Ctrl+X` for exit).
2026-09-05 07:23:18 [gemini-3.8-flash] • A render loop drawing visible rows, a two-line nano-style shortcut bar at the bottom, and
2026-09-05 07:23:18 [gemini-3.8-flash] positioning the cursor.
2026-09-05 07:23:18 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:23:18 [gemini-3.8-flash] Would you like to explore writing a self-contained visual editor module, or look into invoking the
2026-09-05 07:23:18 [gemini-3.8-flash] system's `$EDITOR`?
