2026-09-05 07:33:45 [skid@archlinux] a linux specifiv user account for relay application?
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 gemini-3.8-flash 🌟 🪣 0.7° $0.053 193.4KB 9.39s
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] Creating a dedicated Linux user account for `relay` (or `cog`) is an excellent security and
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] isolation practice, especially when LLMs are given file system access (`submit_file`, forge writes)
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] and prospective shell execution capabilities.
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] Here are the key considerations and steps for setting this up on Arch Linux:
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] Why a Dedicated User Makes Sense
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 1. Blast Radius Containment:
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash]    * An assistant executing arbitrary code, shell commands, or writing files is restricted from
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] touching your primary personal files (`~/.ssh`, browser profiles, personal git configs, GPG keys,
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] and document directories).
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 2. Credential Isolation:
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash]    * Sensitive API tokens (OpenAI, Anthropic, Google) stored in environment variables or
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] `accounts.json` are restricted to that specific user's home directory.
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 3. Clean Environment State:
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash]    * Deno caches, permissions, and session history remain isolated in a known baseline directory.
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] Setup on Arch Linux
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] Since `relay` is an interactive application requiring a TTY, standard shell, and raw mode
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] handling, create a standard unprivileged user with a home directory:
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] # Create the user 'cog' (or 'relay') with a home directory and bash
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] sudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash cog
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] # Set a password if direct login / su is needed
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] sudo passwd cog
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] Shared Development Permissions
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] If your primary desktop user needs read/write access to the relay source tree or forge workspace,
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] add both users to a shared group (or create a dedicated group):
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] # Create a shared developer group
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] sudo groupadd nitrologic
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] sudo usermod -aG nitrologic $USER
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] sudo usermod -aG nitrologic cog
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] # Set appropriate group ownership and setgid on the workspace
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] sudo chown -R :nitrologic /path/to/nitrologic
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] sudo chmod -R g+rwX /path/to/nitrologic
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] sudo find /path/to/nitrologic -type d -exec chmod g+s {} +
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] Interactive Desktop & GUI / Audio Considerations
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] Running an interactive CLI under a secondary user from your primary desktop session introduces a
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] couple of desktop-specific interactions:
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 1. Audio (TTS / Voice Output):
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash]    * Arch Linux typically uses PipeWire. To allow the secondary user to access the primary
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] user's PipeWire sound server without permission errors:
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash]      # When launching under the secondary user:
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash]      export PIPEWIRE_RUNTIME_DIR="/run/user/$(id -u)/"
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash]      Or allow local socket sharing via `/etc/pipewire/` access if needed.
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 2. Clipboard Access (`xclip` / `wl-copy`):
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash]    * Wayland: Pass `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` and `WAYLAND_DISPLAY`:
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash]      sudo -u cog WAYLAND_DISPLAY=$WAYLAND_DISPLAY XDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/$(id -u) cog
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash]    * X11: Authorize local users via `xhost`:
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash]      xhost +si:localuser:cog
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash]      sudo -u cog DISPLAY=$DISPLAY cog
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] Alternative: Lightweight Sandboxing with `bwrap` (Bubblewrap)
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] If managing multiple system users and desktop IPC sockets (audio, display, clipboard) creates
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] unnecessary friction, a common modern Arch approach is running the application under your
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] existing user, but constrained via Bubblewrap (`bubblewrap` in the Arch extra
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] repo):
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] bwrap \
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash]   --ro-bind / / \
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash]   --dev /dev \
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash]   --proc /proc \
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash]   --tmpfs /tmp \
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash]   --bind /home/skid/nitrologic/relay /workspace \
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash]   --chdir /workspace \
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash]   --unshare-all \
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash]   --share-net \
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash]   deno task relay
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] This isolates the process to the project directory without requiring a separate system account or
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] re-wiring PipeWire and display sockets.
2026-09-05 07:33:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:36:02 [skid@archlinux] a relay tool that launches a bubblewrap session sounds good
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 gemini-3.8-flash 🌟 🪣 0.7° $0.050 197.3KB 7.08s
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash] Bubblewrap is ideal for this because it requires no root privileges (uses unprivileged user
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash] namespaces), starts with near-zero latency, and allows exact declarative control over what parts of
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash] the host system are visible.
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash] Design Considerations for a Bubblewrap Tool
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash] 1. Isolation Profile:
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash]    * Root filesystem: Read-only (`--ro-bind / /`), protecting system binaries and shared
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash] libraries while preventing system tampering.
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash]    * Devices & Kernel APIs: Mount a minimal `/dev` (`--dev /dev`) and fresh `/proc`
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash] (`--proc /proc`).
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash]    * Network Access: LLM completion relaying requires network egress (`--share-net`),
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash] whereas local inspection/build sessions can omit it (`--unshare-net`) for total offline
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash] containment.
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash]    * Workspace Isolation: Bind only the designated project or sandbox directory as
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash] read-write (`--bind "$TARGET_DIR" /workspace`), keeping personal keys (`~/.ssh`, `~/.config`),
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash] browser sessions, and other projects invisible.
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash]    * Volatile Scratch: Provide an in-memory `/tmp` (`--tmpfs /tmp`).
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash] 2. Integration Options in Relay:
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash]    * As an LLM Function Call (`bwrap_exec`): The model requests a command to be run. Relay
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash] invokes `bwrap` as a child process via `Deno.Command`, captures stdout/stderr, and returns the
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash] output to the conversation context without risking host state.
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash]    * As an Interactive Console Command (`/bwrap` or `#`): Spawns an interactive shell
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash] (e.g., `bash` or `sh`) inside the container with inherited stdio, dropping you directly into the
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash] sandboxed workspace.
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash] 3. Prerequisites on Arch:
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash]    * Package: `bubblewrap` (available in official Arch repos: `pacman -S bubblewrap`).
2026-09-05 07:36:11 [gemini-3.8-flash]    * Unprivileged user namespaces enabled (default in Arch kernel).
