2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] The crash happens because of a structural mismatch between what `geminiToolCalls` produces and what
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] `relay()` expects when processing tool calls.
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] In `connectGoogle`:
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] `geminiToolCalls` currently packages the call as:
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] {
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 	thought_signature: "call_...",
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 	function_call: {
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 		name: call.name,
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 		args: call.args
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 	}
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] }
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] However, in `relay()`, the dispatch logic adheres to the OpenAI tool-call schema:
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] const toolCalls=calls.map((tool, index) => ({
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 	id: tool.id,
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 	type: "function",
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 	function: { name: tool.function.name, arguments: ... }
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] }));
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] Because `tool.function` is `undefined` (it was keyed as `function_call`), reading
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] `tool.function.name` throws the `TypeError`.
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] The Fix
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] Reverting `geminiToolCalls` to format calls using the normalized schema (which was previously in
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] `oldGeminiToolCalls`) resolves the mismatch:
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Set `id` to `"call_" + (geminiCallCount++)`
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Set `type` to `"function"`
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Set `function` to `{ name: call.name, arguments: JSON.stringify(call.args) }`
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] Switching `connectGoogle` to use `oldGeminiToolCalls` (or updating `geminiToolCalls` to produce
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] that format) will allow the call to pass through `relay()` and `processToolCalls()` cleanly.
2026-09-05 07:41:19 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:44:05 [skid@archlinux] why does deno not work in a vscode session on arch?
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 gemini-3.8-flash 🌟 🪣 0.7° $0.049 202.9KB 5.91s
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] On Arch Linux, Deno failing or misbehaving inside a VS Code session usually comes down to one of
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] four specific environment issues:
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 1. Missing `$PATH` in VS Code's Integrated Terminal / Extension
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] When you launch VS Code from a desktop launcher (rofi, dmenu, GNOME/KDE app menu), the desktop
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] environment often starts VS Code with a minimal default system environment rather than sourcing
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] your login shell's `~/.bashrc`, `~/.zshrc`, or `~/.profile`.
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] • If Deno was installed via the shell installer script (`curl -fsSL https://deno.land/install.sh |
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] sh`), the binary resides in `~/.deno/bin/deno`.
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] • The launcher never sees that path, leading to `"deno: command not found"` or the Deno extension
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] failing to locate the runtime.
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Fix:*
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] • In VS Code settings (`settings.json`):
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash]   "deno.path": "/home/skid/.deno/bin/deno"
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash]   (Or whichever path `which deno` returns when run from your working console).
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Or ensure `~/.deno/bin` is exported in `~/.bash_profile` / `~/.profile` so Wayland/X11 session
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] managers import it upon login.
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 2. The Official Deno Extension Is Not Explicitly Enabled
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] Unlike Node/TypeScript, the official Deno extension for VS Code is disabled by default in
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] workspaces until explicitly turned on, causing red squiggly lines or standard TypeScript compiler
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] errors (e.g. `Cannot find module 'jsr:@...'`).
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Fix:*
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Run the command: `Deno: Initialize Workspace Configuration` (`Ctrl+Shift+P`), or add to
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] `.vscode/settings.json`:
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash]   {
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash]     "deno.enable": true,
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash]     "deno.lint": true,
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash]     "deno.unstable": true
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash]   }
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 3. Missing System Dynamic Libraries (Arch-specific)
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] If you installed Deno via `cargo` or the standalone curl binary on a minimal Arch install, it
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] requires glibc and C++ runtime libraries.
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] • If you see errors like `libstdc++.so.6: cannot open shared object file`, ensure base development
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] libraries are present:
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash]   sudo pacman -S --needed gcc-libs glibc
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Alternatively, install the official Arch package, which links cleanly against system libraries:
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash]   sudo pacman -S deno
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 4. Raw Mode / PTY Injection Issues in VS Code's Terminal
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] Notice in your `relay.ts`:
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] const vscode_nonce=getEnv("VSCODE_NONCE")||getEnv("VSCODE_INJECTION");
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] VS Code's integrated terminal injects shell integration scripts (`VSCODE_INJECTION`) that intercept
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] cursor positions and manipulate the PTY. When a script puts `Deno.stdin.setRaw(true)` into effect,
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] VS Code's terminal wrapper can intercept or mangle control characters (like `Ctrl+C` or escape
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] sequences) differently from a standard terminal emulator like `alacritty`, `foot`, or `kitty`.
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Fix:*
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] If the script hangs or crashes in raw mode inside VS Code, test disabling shell integration in VS
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] Code settings:
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] "terminal.integrated.shellIntegration.enabled": false
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:44:12 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
