2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] If `which deno` returned `command not found`, Deno isn't on your `$PATH` (or isn't installed on the
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] system).
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] Check if it's sitting in the standard local installer location:
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] ls -l ~/.deno/bin/deno
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 1. If it exists in `~/.deno/bin/deno`:
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] Add it to your `$PATH` so VS Code and your shell can see it:
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] echo 'export DENO_INSTALL="$HOME/.deno"' >> ~/.bashrc
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] echo 'export PATH="$DENO_INSTALL/bin:$PATH"' >> ~/.bashrc
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] source ~/.bashrc
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] If you use `zsh`, replace `.bashrc` with `.zshrc`.
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 2. If it is NOT installed at all:
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] On Arch, the cleanest way is to install it via `pacman`:
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] sudo pacman -S deno
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] This places the binary at `/usr/bin/deno`, which is already in every tool's `$PATH` by default,
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] eliminating configuration issues in VS Code.
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] Once installed, verify with:
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] deno --version
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:43 [skid@archlinux] i used arch gui to install not  happy with this
2026-09-05 07:53:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 gemini-3.8-flash 🌟 🪣 0.7° $0.051 212.9KB 11.82s
2026-09-05 07:53:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] GUI software centers on Arch (such as Discover, Pamac, or GNOME Software) often default to
2026-09-05 07:53:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] installing Flatpaks instead of native Arch packages unless explicitly configured otherwise.
2026-09-05 07:53:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] If VS Code was installed as a Flatpak, it is completely sandboxed:
2026-09-05 07:53:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] • It cannot see host binaries like `/usr/bin/deno` or `~/.deno/bin/deno`.
2026-09-05 07:53:55 [gemini-3.8-flash] • The integrated terminal and extensions run inside an isolated mount namespace.
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Standard system tools will report `command not found` inside that environment.
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] 1. Verify if VS Code is a Flatpak
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] Run this in your normal host terminal:
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] flatpak list | grep -i code
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] If you see `com.visualstudio.code` listed, it is running inside a Flatpak container.
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] 2. The Clean Fix (Replace Flatpak with Native Arch Package)
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] To get direct access to system binaries, host environments, and Deno:
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] 1. Remove the Flatpak:
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash]    flatpak uninstall com.visualstudio.code
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash]    (Your workspace files and `~/.config/Code` settings are safe and will persist).
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] 2. Install the Native Arch Package:
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash]    * For the open-source release (from official Arch repos):
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash]      sudo pacman -S code
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash]    * Or, for the official Microsoft branded build (from the AUR):
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash]      yay -S visual-studio-code-bin
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash]      # or: paru -S visual-studio-code-bin
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] Once installed natively, the integrated terminal shares your exact host shell environment, and any
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] `deno` command available in your console will work immediately in VS Code.
2026-09-05 07:53:56 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
