2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] That explains the issue completely: both Visual Studio Code (`com.visualstudio.code`) and
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] VSCodium (`com.vscodium.codium`) are running as Flatpaks.
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] Because they run inside a sandboxed container, they cannot see your host's Deno installation, and
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] when they check for updates, they prompt you to download a `.deb` package because they don't know
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] they are running on Arch.
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] To replace the Flatpak with a native version that has full access to your host tools and Deno:
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 1. Remove the Flatpak:
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash]    flatpak uninstall com.visualstudio.code
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash]    (If you also want to remove VSCodium: `flatpak uninstall com.vscodium.codium`)
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 2. Install the native package via Arch:
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash]    * For standard Arch Code:
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash]      sudo pacman -S code
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash]    * Or for official Microsoft-branded VS Code (via AUR):
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash]      yay -S visual-studio-code-bin
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] Once installed natively, the integrated terminal will directly inherit your system environment, and
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] `deno` will work as expected.
2026-09-05 07:54:48 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
