2026-09-05 08:28:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 gemini-3.8-flash 🌟 🪣 0.7° $0.009 32.79KB 4.53s
2026-09-05 08:28:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] Yes. In Godot, `ClassDB` is both the engine's internal reflection database and an exposed
2026-09-05 08:28:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] singleton within that very database.
2026-09-05 08:28:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 08:28:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] Key aspects of how this works:
2026-09-05 08:28:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 08:28:09 [gemini-3.8-flash] 1. The Role of `ClassDB`:
2026-09-05 08:28:09 [gemini-3.8-flash]    It is the central repository where Godot registers all `Object`-derived classes, along with
2026-09-05 08:28:09 [gemini-3.8-flash] their methods, properties, signals, constants, and inheritance hierarchies. Tools that dump the
2026-09-05 08:28:09 [gemini-3.8-flash] Godot API (such as `--dump-extension-api` for GDExtension) query `ClassDB` directly to produce
2026-09-05 08:28:09 [gemini-3.8-flash] these lists.
2026-09-05 08:28:09 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 08:28:09 [gemini-3.8-flash] 2. Self-Reference:
2026-09-05 08:28:09 [gemini-3.8-flash]    Because `ClassDB` is itself an engine class derived from `Object`, it gets registered into the
2026-09-05 08:28:09 [gemini-3.8-flash] class hierarchy alongside every other class. This is why `ClassDB` appears inside the `"object"`
2026-09-05 08:28:09 [gemini-3.8-flash] list in your file.
2026-09-05 08:28:09 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 08:28:09 [gemini-3.8-flash] 3. Separation from Variants:
2026-09-05 08:28:09 [gemini-3.8-flash]    Notice that the `"variant"` types (such as `Vector3`, `Transform3D`, `Color`, `Array`) are
2026-09-05 08:28:09 [gemini-3.8-flash] listed separately from `"node"`, `"resource"`, and `"object"`. This aligns with Godot's internal
2026-09-05 08:28:09 [gemini-3.8-flash] architecture: primitive engine types are handled by the `Variant` system rather than registered as
2026-09-05 08:28:09 [gemini-3.8-flash] `Object` classes inside `ClassDB`.
2026-09-05 08:28:09 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
