2026-09-05 08:45:03 [gemini-3.8-flash] A few observations regarding how Godot handles class hierarchies and data querying in this script:
2026-09-05 08:45:03 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 08:45:03 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Self-inheritance with `ClassDB.is_parent_class`: In Godot, `ClassDB.is_parent_class(a,
2026-09-05 08:45:03 [gemini-3.8-flash] b)` returns `false` when `a == b`. As a result, the base classes `"Node"`, `"Resource"`, and
2026-09-05 08:45:03 [gemini-3.8-flash] `"Object"` themselves might be omitted from their respective arrays unless explicitly checked or
2026-09-05 08:45:03 [gemini-3.8-flash] included.
2026-09-05 08:45:03 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Overlapping categories: Because all engine classes inherit from `Object`, the `objects`
2026-09-05 08:45:03 [gemini-3.8-flash] array will contain nodes, resources, and editor types. If the goal is a mutually exclusive
2026-09-05 08:45:03 [gemini-3.8-flash] classification (for instance, general objects that are neither nodes nor resources), an exclusion
2026-09-05 08:45:03 [gemini-3.8-flash] check may be needed.
2026-09-05 08:45:03 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Editor classes coverage: Checking prefixes (`Editor` and `ResourceImporter`) captures the
2026-09-05 08:45:03 [gemini-3.8-flash] primary editor types, but certain editor-specific classes in tools builds (such as `FileSystemDock`
2026-09-05 08:45:03 [gemini-3.8-flash] or `ScriptEditor`) use different naming conventions. If comprehensive coverage of editor types is
2026-09-05 08:45:03 [gemini-3.8-flash] required, checking against `EditorInterface` or editor modules may be beneficial.
2026-09-05 08:45:03 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Version status: `Engine.get_version_info()` provides `major`, `minor`, and `patch`, but
2026-09-05 08:45:03 [gemini-3.8-flash] also includes a `"status"` key (such as `"stable"` or `"dev"`). If version matching needs to
2026-09-05 08:45:03 [gemini-3.8-flash] distinguish preview builds from releases, that field can be helpful.
2026-09-05 08:45:03 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 08:45:28 [skid@archlinux] can i add description like in online help?
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 gemini-3.8-flash 🌟 🪣 0.7° (undefined+NaN[2275]) 7.012KB 3.08s
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] Yes, you can extract descriptions, though the mechanism depends on where the script is executed.
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] 1. In an Editor Context (EditorPlugin / EditorScript)
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] In tools/editor builds, `EditorHelp` (an internal editor class) handles documentation, but it is
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] not directly exposed to standard GDScript.
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] However, Godot ships with the full XML documentation bundled inside editor builds. The editor
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] parses these XML resources into an internal cache. If running as an `EditorScript`, documentation
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] for classes, methods, properties, and signals is available through the `EditorInterface` and
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] translation/doc utilities, though direct GDScript bindings for raw docstrings from `ClassDB` are
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] intentionally omitted in release builds to keep binary sizes compact.
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] 2. Extracting from the Engine's Extension API / Dump
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] If this script is run via the command line (headless/standalone), the canonical way to obtain the
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] complete documentation dictionary—matching the online manual word-for-word—is via the engine's
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] built-in API dump command:
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] godot --dump-extension-api
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] This generates an `extension_api.json` file containing the full AST of the engine, including:
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Class briefs and descriptions.
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Method arguments, return types, and descriptions.
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Properties, signals, constants, and theme items with their documentation comments.
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] You can then parse this JSON within your script using `FileAccess` and `JSON.parse_string()` to
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] extract and map descriptions directly to your schema entries.
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] 3. Parsing Doc XMLs Directly
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] Godot's documentation is maintained as XML files in the engine repository (`doc/classes/*.xml`). If
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] your tool runs in an environment with access to Godot's source or cloned docs, an `XMLParser` can
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] be used to read the `<brief_description>` and `<description>` tags for each class.
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] Would you like to see an implementation using the `extension_api.json` dump or an `XMLParser`
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] approach?
2026-09-05 08:45:31 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
