2026-09-13 09:35:12 [skid@archlinux] so that will inform style for other things, by being tight yes?
2026-09-13 09:35:15 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪣 0.7° $0.001 27.87KB 2.54s
2026-09-13 09:35:15 [deepseek-flash] Partly — but be honest with yourself about which part.
2026-09-13 09:35:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-13 09:35:15 [deepseek-flash] The line does two jobs:
2026-09-13 09:35:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-13 09:35:15 [deepseek-flash] - States one rule — no inline call chains. That transfers exactly and only that.
2026-09-13 09:35:15 [deepseek-flash] - Signals a register — terse, unhedged, no pleasantries. A reader who sees it will infer
2026-09-13 09:35:15 [deepseek-flash] "this codebase values brevity," and may mimic the style. That's real, but it's a vibe, not a spec.
2026-09-13 09:35:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-13 09:35:15 [deepseek-flash] What it can't do is inform rules it doesn't mention. A reader won't derive "tabs, not
2026-09-13 09:35:15 [deepseek-flash] spaces," "single-statement guards may sit on the `if` line," or "prefer named functions over
2026-09-13 09:35:15 [deepseek-flash] lambdas" from one line about call chains — they'll just guess, and guess inconsistently.
2026-09-13 09:35:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-13 09:35:15 [deepseek-flash] So: tight is good for the rules you actually name. If you want other things covered, they need to
2026-09-13 09:35:15 [deepseek-flash] be named too (or pointed at in a `STYLE.md`). The comment's brevity doesn't widen its scope — it
2026-09-13 09:35:15 [deepseek-flash] just sets the tone for whatever you do spell out.
2026-09-13 09:35:15 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-13 11:47:48 [skid@archlinux] in godot gd how to quit close game
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪣 0.7° $0.000 502B 2.56s
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] In Godot (both 3.x and 4.x) the usual call is `get_tree().quit()` from any node:
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] func quit_game() -> void:
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] 	get_tree().quit()
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] Optionally pass an exit code: `get_tree().quit(0)` for success, non-zero for an error.
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] Wiring it to a button:
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] func _ready() -> void:
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] 	$QuitButton.pressed.connect(quit_game)
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] func quit_game() -> void:
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] 	get_tree().quit()
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] Notes worth knowing:
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] - `SceneTree.quit()` is the correct way; `OS.kill()` is a blunt fallback and generally not needed.
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] - If you want a confirmation step, show a dialog before calling `quit_game()`.
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] - For a clean shutdown you can handle the notification `NOTIFICATION_WM_CLOSE_WINDOW` (window close
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] button) in a node to save data first, then call `get_tree().quit()`.
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] - On the Web export this call is effectively a no-op — the browser tab controls closing, so
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] design a "return to menu" path instead.
2026-09-13 11:47:51 [deepseek-flash] 
