2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 gemini-3.8-flash 🌟 🪣 0.7° $0.007 19.73KB 3.81s
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] Because `/dev/sda` is a mechanical spinning hard drive (HDD), random read/write seek latency is the
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] biggest bottleneck when transferring thousands of small files. Standard recursive tools tend to
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] jump back and forth across sectors.
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] The fastest way to write to a spinning disk is a streaming `tar` pipeline. Reading from the
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] NVMe happens in memory, and writing to the HDD happens sequentially in large blocks, minimizing
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] head movement.
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] The Fastest Command (Sequential Pipeline)
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] sudo tar -C /srv/http/grid -cf - . | sudo tar -C /mnt/grid26 -xf -
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] With a Progress Bar (if `pv` is installed)
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] If you have `pv` installed (`sudo pacman -S pv`), you can see the throughput and volume
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] transferred:
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] sudo tar -C /srv/http/grid -cf - . | pv | sudo tar -C /mnt/grid26 -xf -
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] Why this is faster for spinning disks:
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] 1. Zero head thrashing: It avoids `rsync`'s two-way checking and directory traversing
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] overhead.
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] 2. Preserves everything: Exact ownership (`http:http`), permissions, modes, and symlinks
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] remain intact.
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] 3. Sequential write stream: The Linux page cache and HDD write buffer can write data to
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] disk in continuous track sectors rather than random small sector writes.
2026-09-18 11:04:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
