2026-09-18 15:35:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] Yes, you can change the ownership of those directories, but you will need superuser privileges
2026-09-18 15:35:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] (`sudo`) to do so. Under Linux, standard users cannot reassign ownership of files they do not own,
2026-09-18 15:35:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] nor can they give away ownership of files to others.
2026-09-18 15:35:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:35:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] Before proceeding, please keep a couple of operational details in mind:
2026-09-18 15:35:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:35:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Web Services: On Arch Linux, `http` is the standard system user and group for web servers
2026-09-18 15:35:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] such as Nginx, Apache, or lighttpd. If an active web service needs to write to or serve from these
2026-09-18 15:35:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] directories, changing the owner to `skid` may cause permission errors for the server unless group
2026-09-18 15:35:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] permissions or access control lists (ACLs) are configured accordingly.
2026-09-18 15:35:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] • The `lost+found` Directory: This is a system-created directory used by filesystem
2026-09-18 15:35:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] integrity tools like `fsck` to recover corrupted or orphaned files. It is generally best practice
2026-09-18 15:35:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] to leave this directory owned by `root` (or untouched) rather than including it in a bulk ownership
2026-09-18 15:35:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] change.
2026-09-18 15:35:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:35:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] If you would like the specific commands to safely adjust the ownership while preserving web server
2026-09-18 15:35:47 [gemini-3.8-flash] access, please let me know.
