2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] Yes, since Arch Linux uses systemd, all mount attempts configured in `/etc/fstab` are handled by
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] systemd mount units and recorded in the system journal.
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] Here are the most direct ways to inspect what happened:
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 1. Check the Mount Unit Status
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] systemd automatically generates a unit named after the mount path (slashes become dashes). You can
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] check its current status and recent log messages with:
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] systemctl status mnt-grid26.mount
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 2. View Logs for the Mount Unit
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] To see the full log output specifically for that mount point from the current boot:
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] journalctl -b -u mnt-grid26.mount
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 3. Check for Any Failed Systemd Units
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] If the mount failure caused a system unit to fail during startup, it will appear here:
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] systemctl --failed
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 4. Search the Boot Log for Mount Events
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] To search the entire log of the current boot for references to the device or mount point:
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] journalctl -b | grep -E "sda1|grid26"
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] Common Things to Verify
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Test `/etc/fstab` directly: You can test the entries in `/etc/fstab` without rebooting by
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] unmounting the drive and running:
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash]   sudo umount /mnt/grid26
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash]   sudo mount -a
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash]   `mount -a` mounts everything in `/etc/fstab` that is not already mounted. Any syntax errors or
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] missing paths will immediately output to the terminal.
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Persistent Naming (UUIDs): Device nodes like `/dev/sda1` can shift to `/dev/sdb1` between
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] reboots depending on drive detection order. Using the filesystem's `UUID` in `/etc/fstab` is
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] standard practice on Arch. You can find the UUID using:
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash]   lsblk -f
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-18 15:40:24 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
