2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] Interesting — all permissions and ownership look completely fine there. Every directory is `755`
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] and traversable, and the file itself is readable. So it's not a filesystem/permissions
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] issue.
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] This points to a different, very common `gzip_static` gotcha:
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] • `gzip_static` only serves the `.gz` file if the client's request includes `Accept-Encoding:
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] gzip`. If it doesn't, and no plain (uncompressed) `.json` file exists, Nginx returns 404 instead of
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] falling back.*
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] So the question becomes: is the actual browser request sending `Accept-Encoding: gzip` for that
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] particular request? Most browsers do by default, but it's worth confirming directly.
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] • Test it explicitly:*
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] # Without Accept-Encoding (simulates client not accepting gzip)
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] curl -I https://skid.nz/roagrid/transit/1/3420/11559.json
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] # With Accept-Encoding: gzip
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] curl -I -H "Accept-Encoding: gzip" https://skid.nz/roagrid/transit/1/3420/11559.json
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] If the first returns `404` and the second returns `200` with `Content-Encoding: gzip`, that
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] confirms the theory.
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] • The fix — add `gunzip on;`*
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] This tells nginx to decompress the `.gz` file on-the-fly for clients that don't request
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] gzip, instead of 404ing:
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] location /roagrid/ {
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] 	add_header Access-Control-Allow-Origin *;
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] 	add_header Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable";
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] 	root /mnt/grid26;
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] 	gzip_static on;
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] 	gunzip on;
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] }
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] This requires the `http_gunzip_module` — check it's available:
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] nginx -V 2>&1 | grep -o http_gunzip_module
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] If that's compiled in, adding `gunzip on;` to all three of your `roagrid`, `topogrid` (and
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] optionally `aerialgrid` if you ever add compressed files there) location blocks should resolve the
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] 404 regardless of whether the client sends `Accept-Encoding: gzip`.
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] Try the curl tests first to confirm the diagnosis before changing config — that'll tell us for
2026-09-18 21:30:34 [claude-sonnet-5] certain.
