2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 claude-sonnet-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.013 1.722KB 13.23s
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] Yes, you can raise the open file descriptor limit on Arch. Errno 24 (EMFILE) means your process hit
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] its per-process fd limit, not necessarily a system-wide one, so where you set this depends on how
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] the service is launched.
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] • Check current limits first:*
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] ulimit -Sn
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] ulimit -Hn
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] cat /proc/sys/fs/file-max
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] • If this runs as a systemd service* (likely, given the log format), ulimit changes in your
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] shell won't affect it — you need to set it in the unit file:
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] systemctl edit your-service.service
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] Add:
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] [Service]
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] LimitNOFILE=65536
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] Then:
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] systemctl daemon-reload
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] systemctl restart your-service.service
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] Verify it took effect:
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] cat /proc/$(pidof your_binary)/limits | grep "Max open files"
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] • If launched from a shell/login session*, edit `/etc/security/limits.conf`:
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] your_user    soft    nofile    65536
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] your_user    hard    nofile    65536
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] This requires PAM's `pam_limits` module to be active in the relevant PAM config (usually already
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] the case on Arch), and a fresh login session.
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] • System-wide ceiling* (rarely the bottleneck unless you're running many processes with
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] high per-process limits):
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] sysctl fs.file-max
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] To persist a higher value, add to `/etc/sysctl.d/99-filemax.conf`:
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] fs.file-max = 2097152
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] Then:
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] sysctl --system
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] • One thing worth checking before just raising limits:* if this server is legitimately
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] holding thousands of concurrent connections/files open, raising the limit is the right fix. But if
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] fd usage climbs over time under normal load, it could be a descriptor leak — e.g., file handles
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] from `loadString` not being closed on error paths or after serving the response. Worth checking
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] with:
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] ls -l /proc/$(pidof your_binary)/fd | wc -l
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] If that count grows steadily over time even without more incoming requests, it's a leak rather than
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] a capacity problem.
2026-09-18 22:23:40 [claude-sonnet-5] 
