2026-09-19 10:43:15 [claude-sonnet-5] Why this is better than the old `wstring_convert` approach:
2026-09-19 10:43:15 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-19 10:43:15 [claude-sonnet-5] - No deprecated API, no pragma needed — compiles clean on every standard.
2026-09-19 10:43:15 [claude-sonnet-5] - Thread-safe — no shared/static converter object; every call is independent.
2026-09-19 10:43:15 [claude-sonnet-5] - Correct on all platforms — properly handles the UTF-16 vs UTF-32 `wchar_t` size
2026-09-19 10:43:15 [claude-sonnet-5] difference instead of relying on a facet that assumed one behavior.
2026-09-19 10:43:15 [claude-sonnet-5] - No exceptions on malformed input — invalid sequences are skipped rather than throwing
2026-09-19 10:43:15 [claude-sonnet-5] (you can change that to insert a replacement character `0xFFFD` if you'd prefer strict Unicode
2026-09-19 10:43:15 [claude-sonnet-5] behavior — let me know if you want that variant).
2026-09-19 10:43:15 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-19 10:43:15 [claude-sonnet-5] If you're on Windows specifically and only care about that platform, `MultiByteToWideChar(CP_UTF8,
2026-09-19 10:43:15 [claude-sonnet-5] ...)` is the "native" replacement and slightly more optimized, but the above works everywhere
2026-09-19 10:43:15 [claude-sonnet-5] without `#ifdef`s.
