2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 claude-sonnet-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.009 9.354KB 5.00s
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] `ls` with One Filename Per Line, No Extra Info
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] ls -1
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] The `-1` flag (that's the number one, not a lowercase L) forces single-column output — one
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] name per line, no permissions, sizes, or dates.
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] Note on Piping
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] If you're piping `ls` output to another command, you often don't even need `-1` — `ls`
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] automatically switches to one-per-line when it detects the output isn't a terminal:
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] ls | grep ".js"
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] Truly Bare (No Color, No Indicators)
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] If your shell has `ls` aliased with extra flags (common with `alias ls='ls --color=auto'` or
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] similar), you might still see color codes or symbols like `*`, `/`, `@`. For a completely plain,
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] unaliased result:
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] \ls -1
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] The leading backslash bypasses any alias and calls the real `ls` binary directly.
2026-09-20 09:01:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
