2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] Two Issues Here
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] Issue 1: The Real Error — Dependency Conflict
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] error: removing falkon breaks dependency 'falkon' required by kde-network-meta
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] Falkon is currently required by the `kde-network-meta` metapackage (a bundling package that
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] pulls in a group of network-related apps as part of the Plasma "network" component group). Pacman
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] won't remove a package while another package still depends on it.
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] Fix: Remove Both Together
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] Since `kde-network-meta` is just a metapackage (no real files of its own, just dependency links),
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] it's safe to remove alongside Falkon:
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] sudo pacman -Rns falkon kde-network-meta
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] This won't break your KDE desktop — metapackages exist only to group installs, not to provide
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] functionality themselves.
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] Alternative: Force Remove (Skip Dependency Check)
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] If you specifically want to keep `kde-network-meta` installed for some reason (not usually
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] necessary):
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] sudo pacman -Rdd falkon
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] ⚠️ Use this cautiously — `-dd` skips all dependency checks, so only do this if you're sure
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] it won't break anything else.
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] Issue 2: That Weird `^[[200~` Text
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] bash: $'\E[200~sudo': command not found
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] This is a bracketed paste mode artifact. It happens when you paste text into the terminal
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] and your terminal emulator wraps it in special escape codes (`\E[200~` ... `\E[201~`) to tell bash
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] "this is pasted text, not typed." Sometimes bash/terminal combos mishandle this, and the raw escape
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] codes leak into the command line.
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] Quick Fix: Disable Bracketed Paste in Bash
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] Add this to your `~/.bashrc`:
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] bind 'set enable-bracketed-paste off'
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] Then reload:
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] source ~/.bashrc
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] Alternative: Just Retype Instead of Pasting
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] If this only happens occasionally, simply typing the command manually (like you did successfully
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] the second time) avoids the issue entirely.
2026-09-20 09:37:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
