2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] Fair enough — no need to excuse anything. You're right that "unused software = larger attack
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] surface" is a legitimate security principle, not paranoia. Every installed package is something
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] that could theoretically have a vulnerability, and "just leave it, it's harmless" isn't great
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] security advice even if the immediate risk is low. Let's get it actually gone.
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] Removing Falkon Properly
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] Step 1: Remove Falkon and the Metapackage Together
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] sudo pacman -Rns falkon kde-network-meta
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] This clears the dependency conflict since you're removing both at once. The `-n` also nukes
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] leftover config files, and `-s` cleans up orphaned dependencies that only existed to support
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] Falkon.
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] Step 2: Verify It's Actually Gone
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] pacman -Qs falkon
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] No output = fully removed.
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] Step 3: Clean Up Any Leftover User Config
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] rm -rf ~/.config/falkon ~/.cache/falkon
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] Step 4: Check for Orphaned Dependencies
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] Sometimes removing one package leaves "orphans" — packages that were only pulled in as
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] dependencies:
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] pacman -Qtdq
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] If it lists anything, review it and remove with:
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] sudo pacman -Rns $(pacman -Qtdq)
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] About `kde-network-meta`
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] Worth knowing: removing that metapackage won't break your existing network tools (already-installed
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] apps stay installed). It just means future `pacman -Syu` won't automatically pull in whatever new
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] apps get added to that group later. If you want tighter control over what's installed anyway,
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] that's arguably a net positive for your threat model, not a downside.
2026-09-20 09:40:21 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:42:58 [skid@archlinux] so attack vector increased without my permission?
2026-09-20 09:43:12 [claude-sonnet-5] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 claude-sonnet-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.026 21.74KB 13.87s
2026-09-20 09:43:12 [claude-sonnet-5] Yes — That's Exactly What Happened, and It's a Fair Complaint
2026-09-20 09:43:12 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:12 [claude-sonnet-5] How This Occurs
2026-09-20 09:43:12 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:12 [claude-sonnet-5] Metapackages like `kde-network-meta` are dependency bundles. When you (or an
2026-09-20 09:43:12 [claude-sonnet-5] installer/installation script) installed something like `plasma-meta` or a KDE group package, it
2026-09-20 09:43:12 [claude-sonnet-5] silently pulled in a whole tree of "recommended" apps — Falkon included — without asking you
2026-09-20 09:43:12 [claude-sonnet-5] individually about each one.
2026-09-20 09:43:12 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:12 [claude-sonnet-5] This is a real, well-known criticism of meta-package-driven installs: you get convenience
2026-09-20 09:43:12 [claude-sonnet-5] at the cost of implicit consent. You didn't choose Falkon. A packaging decision chose it
2026-09-20 09:43:12 [claude-sonnet-5] for you.
2026-09-20 09:43:12 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:12 [claude-sonnet-5] Why This Happens on Arch (and Similar Distros)
2026-09-20 09:43:12 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:12 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:12 [claude-sonnet-5] ╭──────────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-09-20 09:43:12 [claude-sonnet-5] │  Install Method               │  Consent Level                    │
2026-09-20 09:43:12 [claude-sonnet-5] ├──────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-09-20 09:43:12 [claude-sonnet-5] │  sudo pacman -S falkon        │  Explicit — you chose it           │
2026-09-20 09:43:12 [claude-sonnet-5] │  sudo pacman -S plasma-meta   │  Implicit — bundled dependency     │
2026-09-20 09:43:12 [claude-sonnet-5] │  Distro's default ISO/install │  Zero — decided before you existed │
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] ╰──────────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] Arch is supposed to be minimal and user-controlled compared to Ubuntu/Fedora, but KDE's own
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] meta-packages still carry this "batteries included" philosophy from upstream KDE, not from Arch
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] itself.
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] How to Prevent This Going Forward
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 1. Inspect Before Installing Meta-packages
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] pacman -Si kde-network-meta
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] Look at the Depends On field before you install a metapackage — you'll see the full list
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] before it lands on your system.
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 2. Use `--asdeps` Awareness
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] Check whether something was pulled in as a dependency vs. explicitly requested:
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] pacman -Qi falkon | grep "Install Reason"
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] If it says `Installed as a dependency`, that confirms it was never something you asked for
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] directly.
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 3. Avoid Broad Meta-packages Going Forward
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] Instead of:
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] sudo pacman -S plasma-meta
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] Consider a more surgical install:
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] sudo pacman -S plasma-desktop konsole dolphin
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] You lose some convenience, but you gain explicit control over exactly what touches your
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] system — which sounds like exactly what you want.
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] Bottom Line
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] You're not wrong to be annoyed. "It came bundled" is a legitimate root cause, not user error. The
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] fix isn't just removing Falkon once — it's changing how you install KDE components going
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] forward so this doesn't keep happening silently.
2026-09-20 09:43:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:39 [skid@archlinux] when app like discord opens image in arch where does it get default app from?
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 claude-sonnet-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.008 546B 8.33s
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] Default Application Resolution in Arch Linux
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] When an app like Discord opens an image, the resolution generally follows this chain:
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 1. MIME Type Associations (XDG)
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] Most desktop apps use the XDG MIME Applications specification. The lookup order is
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] typically:
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] - `~/.config/mimeapps.list` (user overrides)
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] - `/etc/xdg/mimeapps.list` (system-wide overrides)
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] - `~/.local/share/applications/mimeapps.list`
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] - `/usr/share/applications/mimeapps.list` (defaults set by packages)
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] These files map MIME types (e.g., `image/png`) to `.desktop` file names.
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 2. The `.desktop` Files
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] Once a MIME type is matched to an application ID, the system looks in:
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] - `~/.local/share/applications/`
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] - `/usr/share/applications/`
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] - `/usr/local/share/applications/`
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] for a matching `.desktop` file, which contains the actual `Exec=` line telling the system what
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] binary to run.
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 3. Tools That Do the Lookup
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] Discord (an Electron app) typically shells out to `xdg-open`, which:
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 1. Checks `mimeapps.list` for the MIME type
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 2. Falls back to querying `xdg-mime query default <mimetype>`
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 3. Executes the resolved `.desktop` entry
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] Checking/Changing It Yourself
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] You can inspect and modify these associations with:
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] # Check current default for image/png
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] xdg-mime query default image/png
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] # Set a new default
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] xdg-mime default org.gnome.gimp.desktop image/png
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] Or manually edit `~/.config/mimeapps.list`:
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] [Default Applications]
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] image/png=feh.desktop
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] image/jpeg=feh.desktop
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] Desktop Environment Overrides
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] If you're running GNOME, KDE, or another DE, their Settings → Default Applications panel
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] usually just writes to the same `mimeapps.list` file, so changes there and via `xdg-mime` are
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] interchangeable.
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] If Discord is opening something unexpected, checking `~/.config/mimeapps.list` first is usually the
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] fastest way to diagnose it.
2026-09-20 09:46:48 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:47:35 [skid@archlinux] [skid@archlinux craze]$ xdg-mime query default image/png
2026-09-20 09:47:35 [skid@archlinux] org.kde.gwenview.desktop
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 claude-sonnet-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.007 2.728KB 6.57s
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] That confirms Gwenview (KDE's image viewer) is set as your default handler for PNG images.
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] If Discord is opening PNGs in Gwenview and you want to switch to something else (e.g., `feh`,
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] `imv`, `GIMP`), you have a couple of options:
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] Option A: Use `xdg-mime` to set a new default
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] xdg-mime default feh.desktop image/png
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] You'll need the correct `.desktop` file name — check available ones with:
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] ls /usr/share/applications/ | grep -i feh
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] Option B: Edit `mimeapps.list` directly
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] nano ~/.config/mimeapps.list
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] Add or edit under `[Default Applications]`:
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] image/png=feh.desktop
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] If you want all image types to change, not just PNG
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] You'd want to repeat this for each relevant MIME type:
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] xdg-mime default feh.desktop image/png
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] xdg-mime default feh.desktop image/jpeg
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] xdg-mime default feh.desktop image/gif
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] xdg-mime default feh.desktop image/webp
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] Let me know what you're trying to switch it to (or if Discord isn't actually respecting this
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] setting — Electron apps sometimes have their own internal handling that bypasses XDG), and I can
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] help narrow it down further.
2026-09-20 09:47:42 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:48:11 [skid@archlinux] i dunno, it is actually opening default browser falkon
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 claude-sonnet-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.012 4.152KB 11.59s
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] That makes sense actually — it's likely not an image MIME type issue at all. Here's why:
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] The Likely Cause: URL vs File
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] Discord images are usually hosted on their CDN (`cdn.discordapp.com` or `media.discordapp.net`).
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] When you click an image in Discord, it often doesn't download it locally first — instead it just
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] opens the URL in your default browser.
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] That means the relevant setting isn't `image/png`, it's the URL scheme handler:
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] xdg-mime query default x-scheme-handler/https
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] xdg-mime query default x-scheme-handler/http
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] If that returns `org.kde.falkon.desktop`, that's your culprit.
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] Why This Happens
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] - Right-click → "Copy Image" or clicking to preview within Discord = stays in-app
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] - Clicking "Open in Browser", or certain link/image previews = uses your default
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] browser, not your default image viewer
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] - This is normal Discord behavior — it doesn't always download-then-open a local file
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] What You Can Do
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] • Option 1: Accept it (probably intended)*
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] If you're clicking something like "Open in Browser," this behavior is expected — image mime
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] handlers won't apply.
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] • Option 2: Right-click → Save/Download, then open manually*
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] This avoids the browser entirely.
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] • Option 3: Switch default browser* (if this is a broader annoyance)
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] xdg-settings set default-web-browser org.kde.falkon.desktop
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] (swap `falkon` for whichever browser you actually want as default)
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-20 09:48:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
