2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] CORS as a concept predates Mr. Robot by several years.
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] Timeline
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] ╭──────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] │  Year    │  Milestone                                                │
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] ├──────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] │  2009    │  W3C begins CORS Working Draft                            │
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] │  2010    │  Firefox 3.5 adds early CORS support                      │
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] │  2011    │  Chrome/WebKit implement `crossorigin` attribute on <img> │
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] │  2014    │  CORS becomes official W3C Recommendation                 │
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] │  2015    │  📺 Mr. Robot premieres (June 2015)                       │
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] ╯──────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] So by the time Mr. Robot aired, `crossOrigin`/CORS had already been a finalized W3C standard for
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] about a year and a half, and browser support had existed for 3–4 years before that.
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] Why It Was Created
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] Worth noting the reason it exists ties into browser security history more broadly — CORS
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] was designed to relax the Same-Origin Policy (which itself dates back to Netscape Navigator
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 2.0 in 1995) in a controlled way, so that legitimate cross-origin requests (APIs, CDNs, fonts,
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] etc.) could happen safely without opening the door to the kind of data-exfiltration attacks
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] Same-Origin Policy was originally meant to prevent.
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] So the timeline is roughly: 1995 Same-Origin Policy → 2009–2014 CORS spec development → 2015
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] Mr. Robot. The mechanism you're using in `dsptool` today is essentially unchanged from what was
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] standardized a decade before the show existed.
2026-09-20 11:13:19 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 11:13:39 [skid@archlinux] in particular the term anonymous
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 claude-sonnet-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.380 519.2KB 13.41s
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] The term "anonymous" in this context refers specifically to the credential state of
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] the request — not anything about hiding identity from the destination server in a broader sense.
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] What It Actually Means
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] When you set:
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] image.crossOrigin = "anonymous";
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] The browser sends the cross-origin request without:
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] - Cookies
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] - HTTP authentication headers
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] - TLS client certificates
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] So from the server's point of view, it can't tie the request back to a specific logged-in
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] user session — the request arrives "anonymously," credential-wise. That's the literal sense the
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] spec authors meant: not "untraceable," just "no identifying credentials attached."
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] Compare with the other option:
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] image.crossOrigin = "use-credentials";
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] This explicitly does send cookies/auth — the opposite of anonymous.
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] Why This Distinction Mattered for the Spec
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] The security model CORS was solving wasn't just "can Origin A fetch a resource from Origin B" — it
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] was also "should Origin A's page be able to read personalized data from Origin B using the
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] visitor's existing login session at B." Naming the default mode "anonymous" made the
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] credential-stripping behavior explicit and self-documenting in code, rather than requiring a
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] doc-lookup to understand what the default request behavior would be.
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] On the Deeper Etymology
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] I don't have solid documented sourcing on the exact WHATWG/W3C working-group discussion where
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] "anonymous" specifically (versus some alternative term) got chosen and bikeshedded — I'd be
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] guessing if I claimed a specific rationale beyond the semantic one above. If you want, I can try to
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] dig for the actual spec issue/PR history, but I don't want to invent a tidier origin story than
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] what's actually documented.
2026-09-20 11:13:53 [claude-sonnet-5] 
