2026-09-20 11:51:44 [claude-sonnet-5] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.3 claude-sonnet-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.008 6.427KB 6.61s
2026-09-20 11:51:44 [claude-sonnet-5] Fair enough — hash-based is solid until the one thing that actually breaks it: stale HTML
2026-09-20 11:51:44 [claude-sonnet-5] referencing an old hash after you've removed that file.
2026-09-20 11:51:44 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 11:51:44 [claude-sonnet-5] The usual pattern to avoid that:
2026-09-20 11:51:44 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 11:51:44 [claude-sonnet-5] - Keep old hashed files around for a while (don't delete immediately) or
2026-09-20 11:51:44 [claude-sonnet-5] - Make sure your HTML/deploy step always updates in lockstep with the new hash, so there's no
2026-09-20 11:51:44 [claude-sonnet-5] window where a page references a hash that's already gone
2026-09-20 11:51:44 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 11:51:44 [claude-sonnet-5] As long as the HTML and the hashed asset are deployed together, it holds up fine indefinitely. It
2026-09-20 11:51:44 [claude-sonnet-5] only "breaks" if something references a version that's already been purged/deleted — which is a
2026-09-20 11:51:44 [claude-sonnet-5] deploy discipline issue, not a CDN one.
2026-09-20 11:51:44 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-09-20 11:51:44 [claude-sonnet-5] Good plan either way — full purge now, hash paths later when you're ready.
