2026-09-20 13:45:43 [gpt-5.6-sol] Chrome is probably not requesting the `.gz` path. It requests:
2026-09-20 13:45:43 [gpt-5.6-sol] 
2026-09-20 13:45:43 [gpt-5.6-sol] `/.well-known/appspecific/com.chrome.devtools.json`
2026-09-20 13:45:43 [gpt-5.6-sol] 
2026-09-20 13:45:43 [gpt-5.6-sol] and advertises gzip support via `Accept-Encoding`. Your web server then probes for a pre-compressed
2026-09-20 13:45:43 [gpt-5.6-sol] `.gz` sidecar.
2026-09-20 13:45:43 [gpt-5.6-sol] 
2026-09-20 13:45:43 [gpt-5.6-sol] So the preferable fix is server-side: disable pre-compressed-file probing, or make a missing `.gz`
2026-09-20 13:45:43 [gpt-5.6-sol] file fall back silently to the uncompressed file.
2026-09-20 13:45:43 [gpt-5.6-sol] 
2026-09-20 13:45:43 [gpt-5.6-sol] If you want to stop Chrome’s original request instead, check DevTools Settings → Workspace
2026-09-20 13:45:43 [gpt-5.6-sol] (or Preferences → Sources, depending on Chrome version) and disable Automatically
2026-09-20 13:45:43 [gpt-5.6-sol] connect to workspace folders. You can also search DevTools settings for “workspace”.
2026-09-20 13:45:43 [gpt-5.6-sol] 
2026-09-20 13:45:43 [gpt-5.6-sol] Disabling gzip/content encoding in Chrome would affect all resources and is not recommended merely
2026-09-20 13:45:43 [gpt-5.6-sol] to suppress this harmless lookup.
