2026-09-21 10:18:39 [gpt-5.6-sol] A few possibilities—even though ImageMagick can read its header:
2026-09-21 10:18:39 [gpt-5.6-sol] 
2026-09-21 10:18:39 [gpt-5.6-sol] - The JPEG is truncated or has malformed data.
2026-09-21 10:18:39 [gpt-5.6-sol] - It contains an unusual colour profile or metadata.
2026-09-21 10:18:39 [gpt-5.6-sol] - Discord’s client failed while reading/uploading the file.
2026-09-21 10:18:39 [gpt-5.6-sol] - The upload is being altered by a browser extension, proxy, or network issue.
2026-09-21 10:18:39 [gpt-5.6-sol] - Less likely: Discord is struggling with the `4096 × 4096` image during preview generation. Its
2026-09-21 10:18:39 [gpt-5.6-sol] `5.2 MiB` size should normally be acceptable.
2026-09-21 10:18:39 [gpt-5.6-sol] 
2026-09-21 10:18:39 [gpt-5.6-sol] A clean re-encode often fixes it:
2026-09-21 10:18:39 [gpt-5.6-sol] 
2026-09-21 10:18:39 [gpt-5.6-sol] 
2026-09-21 10:18:39 [gpt-5.6-sol] magick ~/Desktop/3774_10997.jpg -auto-orient -strip -colorspace sRGB ~/Desktop/3774_10997-clean.jpg
2026-09-21 10:18:39 [gpt-5.6-sol] 
2026-09-21 10:18:39 [gpt-5.6-sol] 
2026-09-21 10:18:39 [gpt-5.6-sol] Then upload the `-clean.jpg` version. To check whether the original JPEG is damaged:
2026-09-21 10:18:39 [gpt-5.6-sol] 
2026-09-21 10:18:39 [gpt-5.6-sol] 
2026-09-21 10:18:39 [gpt-5.6-sol] magick identify -regard-warnings ~/Desktop/3774_10997.jpg
2026-09-21 10:18:39 [gpt-5.6-sol] 
2026-09-21 10:18:39 [gpt-5.6-sol] 
2026-09-21 10:18:39 [gpt-5.6-sol] If the clean copy also reports as empty, try Discord in a browser or another client; that would
2026-09-21 10:18:39 [gpt-5.6-sol] point more toward an upload/client issue than the image itself.
