2026-09-22 08:48:42 [gpt-5.6-sol] Yes—Deno can safely call Bunny.net APIs using an API key, provided the key stays
2026-09-22 08:48:42 [gpt-5.6-sol] server-side.
2026-09-22 08:48:42 [gpt-5.6-sol] 
2026-09-22 08:48:42 [gpt-5.6-sol] - Store it in a Deno environment variable or your hosting platform’s secret manager.
2026-09-22 08:48:42 [gpt-5.6-sol] - Do not embed it in browser JavaScript, HTML, public repositories, or responses.
2026-09-22 08:48:42 [gpt-5.6-sol] - Use the appropriate Bunny credential: account API key, Storage Zone password, or
2026-09-22 08:48:42 [gpt-5.6-sol] token-authentication key.
2026-09-22 08:48:42 [gpt-5.6-sol] - Prefer a narrowly scoped credential where Bunny supports one. The main account API key may have
2026-09-22 08:48:42 [gpt-5.6-sol] broad access.
2026-09-22 08:48:42 [gpt-5.6-sol] - Send requests from your Deno backend using `fetch`.
2026-09-22 08:48:42 [gpt-5.6-sol] - If using Deno Deploy, configure the key as a project secret.
2026-09-22 08:48:42 [gpt-5.6-sol] - For client downloads, generate short-lived signed URLs/tokens server-side rather than exposing
2026-09-22 08:48:42 [gpt-5.6-sol] the key.
2026-09-22 08:48:42 [gpt-5.6-sol] 
2026-09-22 08:48:42 [gpt-5.6-sol] If a key has already appeared in client code or a repository, rotate it in Bunny’s dashboard.
2026-09-22 08:48:42 [gpt-5.6-sol] 
