2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] Based on this Nginx configuration, here is how long your data will last in the Bunny.net cache:
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] 1. The Static Grid Locations (Will last weeks to months)
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] For paths such as `/aerialgrid/`, `/topogrid2/`, `/sd-aerialgrid/`, etc., your origin sends:
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] add_header Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable";
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Explicit Expiration: You are instructing Bunny.net that these files are valid for 1
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] year (`31,536,000` seconds) and will never change (`immutable`).
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Retention: Bunny.net will not expire these assets on its own schedule. They will persist
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] in the edge cache across days and weeks.
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] • The Only Eviction Factor: If a specific regional POP receives very few requests for a
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] given tile or file, it may eventually be evicted via LRU (Least Recently Used) to free disk space
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] on that specific edge server. In high-traffic POPs (or if your user base is consistently active),
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] they will stay cached for the long term.
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] 2. The Dynamic / Root Proxy (Will NOT last)
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] For requests falling through to `location /` (proxied to `localhost:8083`):
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] • This block does not set any explicit `Cache-Control` header.
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Unless your backend process listening on `:8083` emits explicit caching headers, Bunny.net will
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] treat these responses as uncacheable or will cache them only for very short heuristic windows.
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] A Note on Your `add_header` Directives
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] In Nginx, the `add_header` directive does not inherit across blocks if child blocks define
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] their own `add_header`. Because you define `add_header Cache-Control` inside each `/...grid/`
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] location, the outer `Strict-Transport-Security` header is dropped for those specific
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] locations.
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] If you want HSTS sent on those static grid assets as well, you must duplicate that directive inside
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] each location block.
2026-09-23 07:13:36 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-23 07:15:04 [skid@archlinux] would 2 hours be ok for root?
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 gemini-3.8-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.003 16.61KB 3.28s
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] Yes, 2 hours (`max-age=7200`) is often a very practical middle ground for dynamic
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] applications.
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] Before implementing it, consider how your root application behaves:
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] When 2 Hours Works Well
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Semi-static assets: HTML shells, dynamic CSS bundles, scripts, or landing pages that
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] update periodically but not constantly.
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Database / Backend relief: It will shield your backend on `:8083` from repeated hits for
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] popular routes, serving those requests directly from the Bunny edge.
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Acceptable invalidation window: If you push a code update, you can either wait up to 2
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] hours for it to naturally propagate, or invoke your purge script (as in your first task) to force
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] an immediate refresh.
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] When 2 Hours Might Cause Issues
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] • Personalized / Session-based data: If responses differ per user (e.g., user profiles,
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] login state, shopping carts, or set cookies), a public 2-hour cache will cause users to see each
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] other’s cached content.
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] • State mutation APIs: POST/PUT/DELETE endpoints or dynamic JSON feeds that rely on
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] immediate consistency.
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] Implementation Options
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] If you determine that your root endpoint is safe to cache publicly, you have two primary ways to
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] configure it:
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 1. At the Origin (Nginx):
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash]    Add the header directly inside `location /`:
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash]    location / {
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash]        proxy_pass http://backend;
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash]        proxy_http_version 1.1;
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash]        proxy_set_header Connection "keep-alive";
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash]        proxy_set_header X-Host $host;
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash]        proxy_set_header X-Remote $remote_addr;
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash]        add_header Cache-Control "public, max-age=7200";
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash]    }
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash]    (Alternatively, configure your backend application on `:8083` to emit this header).
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 2. At the CDN Level (Bunny.net Edge Rules):
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash]    If you want browser clients to revalidate more frequently than the CDN, you can configure
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] headers such as `Cache-Control: public, max-age=300, s-maxage=7200`. This instructs end-user
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] browsers to cache for only 5 minutes, while Bunny.net keeps the edge copy fresh for 2 hours.
2026-09-23 07:15:08 [gemini-3.8-flash] 
