2026-09-24 08:34:33 [gpt-6-sol] Chromium or Chrome through `taskset`, choosing the CPU numbers you want—for example, 0–3 for four
2026-09-24 08:34:33 [gpt-6-sol] logical CPUs. Close Chrome completely first; otherwise a new launch may hand the window to an
2026-09-24 08:34:33 [gpt-6-sol] already-running, unrestricted process.
2026-09-24 08:34:33 [gpt-6-sol] 
2026-09-24 08:34:33 [gpt-6-sol] For a persistent setup in KDE, edit Chrome’s application launcher in KDE Menu Editor so it
2026-09-24 08:34:33 [gpt-6-sol] starts through `taskset`.
2026-09-24 08:34:33 [gpt-6-sol] 
2026-09-24 08:34:33 [gpt-6-sol] This limits which CPUs Chrome can use, not its total CPU percentage. If you mean “never use
2026-09-24 08:34:33 [gpt-6-sol] more than a certain amount of CPU,” that needs a cgroup CPU quota instead.
