2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.003 1.947KB 11.53s
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] A few observations, mostly small.
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] • Correct as written*
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] - Resolving each pattern against the zone with `new URL(pattern, base)` is sound, and the trailing
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] slash on `purgeZone` is required for it to behave as a base rather than a file path. `*` is not in
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] the path or form-urlencoded percent-encode sets, so it survives both the URL parse and the
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] `searchParams` serialisation intact — the wildcard reaches Bunny unescaped.
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] - The `AccessKey` header name and the `url`/`async` query parameters match Bunny's purge endpoint.
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] - Sequential `await` in the loop is fine, and `async=false` means each call blocks until the purge
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] completes, so ordering is deterministic.
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] • Difference worth confirming*
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] The commented-out patterns at the top were `dsp`, `wire`, `dsptool.css`, but the live
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] `patterns` array is `nitro`, `wire`, `dsptool.css`. If `dsp` → `nitro` was the
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] intended edit, fine; if it was a scratch change, the old `dsp*` set is now unpurged.
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] • Robustness*
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] - `./apikey` resolves against the process working directory, not the module. Running the script
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] from elsewhere will fail with a confusing `NotFound`. Anchoring it to `import.meta.url` (or
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] `import.meta.dirname`) makes it location-independent.
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] - Nothing catches a mid-loop failure, so a single non-OK response aborts the remaining patterns
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] with a partial purge. That's a defensible fail-fast choice, but if all three need to be attempted,
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] collect failures and report at the end.
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] - If you purge often enough to hit Bunny's rate limiting, a short delay between calls (or retry on
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] 429) would help. I don't want to quote a specific limit since it may have changed.
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] - The error path reads the body as text; Bunny typically returns JSON, so if you want to
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] distinguish validation errors from transient ones, parse it.
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] • Style*
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] - `): Promise<void>{` is missing the space before the brace that the rest of the file uses.
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] - The commented-out pattern lines are now dead and duplicated by the array; worth deleting.
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] - The function takes the API key per call. It's only ever called with the same value, so a
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] module-level constant or a closure over the key would read more cleanly.
2026-09-25 22:08:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.008 419.9KB 16.97s
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] A substantial codebase — thanks for sharing. A few observations, grouped by what stands out and
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] what looks risky.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] Architecture notes
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] The tile system (`TopoGrid` / `TopoTile` / `TopoSource`) is cleanly layered: source→tile→baker
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] worker→bumpmap→surface. The `Bumpmap` topo/diff/ground triple with
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] `pickElevation`/`pickHeight`/`pickGround` separation is a nice way to keep elevation and detail
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] separate — it lets the surface crack logic (`CreateTopoSurface`) keep detail verts only where
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] `ground==0 && maxz-minz>12`.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] `Bits` is a tidy little polygon rasterizer using 4×4 packed cells; the `digTopo` / `cropTopo`
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] variants read well.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] The `Shape` instancing layout (16 shorts per instance: 4 floats xyzw + 9 rotated matrix as shorts +
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] 3 dim) packed into a single `Int16Array` is efficient, but I'd worry about the `flat` attribute
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] stride computation in `bindAttributes` — it walks `attributes` twice and relies on `flat`-prefixed
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] names appearing after non-flat ones in the same object. If insertion order ever changes that will
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] silently corrupt the VAO.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] The `Arpeggiator` progression table with mythology names is charming and the state-machine in
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] `Beat()` is compact.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] Things that look like real bugs
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] - `Pixelmap` constructor never writes to `this.rgb` — the loop reads `bytes` and advances `p`, but
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] `rgb` is populated nowhere. The class is effectively a no-op.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] - `wire_topo.js` `TopoTile.cropOutline` calls `outlinePoints(outline)` but the method defined is
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] `outlinePoints()` with no parameters and it returns `Bits` — signature mismatch.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] - `TopoTile.drawOutline` references `this.resolution` and `this.pickHeight(...)`, neither of which
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] exist on `TopoTile` (they live on `heightmap`).
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] - `wire_topo.js` `fetchDrawing2` uses undefined `data` and `this.grid`.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] - `wire_util.js` `EventServer.addEvent` assigns to an undeclared `event` (implicit global). Same
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] pattern in `worker_post.js`.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] - `worker-vsynth.js` `Keys.ToJson` is missing a comma between `this.lowhigh[1]` and
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] `this.lowhigh[2]` — concatenation, not array.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] - `wire_grid.js` `Panel.constructor` calls `this.addKey(...)` before `addKey` is defined on the
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] class? Actually it's fine because methods are on the prototype at construction time; but
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] `this.addKey` internally calls `this._clickKey` via `bind`, which is also fine. No bug here, just
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] worth noting it's only safe because of prototype timing.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] - `wire_display.js` `CombineSurfaces` is empty — every `Frame()`/`Hull()`/`MiniWheel()` path
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] returns `undefined`.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] - `Shape.addFlatSurface` calls `this.instanceBuffer(shorts, this.instances, false)` with three
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] args, but `instanceBuffer(shorts, instanceCount)` takes two. The third is silently dropped.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] - `wire_grid.js` `TopoEditor.onTiles` uses `console.log("TopoEditor.onTiles")` but `TopoSource`
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] constructor calls `this.fetchMasks()` — and then `TopoDisplay.onTopoTileSource` also seems to
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] expect a different shape. The two tile pipelines (`TopoDisplay` in wire_display vs `TopoEditor` in
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] wire_grid) look like they've diverged.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] - `wire_geometry.js` `SuperSurface.extrudeEdge` does `t=Normalize(t)` on a local `const t` —
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] reassignment of a const, which would throw in strict mode. Same file, `drill()` references
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] `addEdge`/`addWall` that don't exist.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] - `worker-vsynth.js` `Reverb.EffectAudio` has `this.future = this.future.slice(0, maxOffset)` which
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] truncates when `future.length < maxOffset` — that condition should be growing, not slicing.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] Probably should be `if (this.future.length < maxOffset) { extend }`.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] - `worker-vsynth.js` `Stream.Feed` compares `count > this.count + 1` and stashes into `reorder`,
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] but the loop only ever consumes `count+1` once — there's no drain for the stashed entries beyond a
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] single step. If you get two out-of-order packets it will wedge.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] Style / consistency
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] The "simon come here" markers are a good habit, but several mark real correctness gaps (worker
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] fallback, `_MeshLoader` null deref, retirement sanity). Might be worth a `// TODO(simon):`
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] convention so they show up in tooling.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] Indentation is inconsistent across files: most wire_*.js use tabs, but `wire_topo.js`'s `TopoTile`
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] mixes tabs and 8-space continuation lines inside `drawFilled`/`drawOutline`. Not fatal but noisy in
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] diffs.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] Smaller things
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] - `wire_system.js` `failVR()` takes no parameter but logs `vr.length`.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] - `wire_field.js` `testPost()` references `verts`, `indices`, and `postMessage` as if in a worker
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] context, but the file is a worker — the function is dead code referencing outer-scope names
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] that don't exist.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] - `Bumpmap.lakeLine` declares `const g=this.ground` unused.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] - `wire_topo.js` `Bumpmap.processOutline` calls `contract()` then three `expand()`s — comment says
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] outline, but the effect is a dilate; name is misleading.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] - `wire_display.js` `TopoDisplay.onTopoTileSource` reads `this.tilesource` and `this.loadingTile`
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] but nothing appears to set `this.loadingTile` in the display class itself (it's set in `fetchTile`,
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] which is only called from `onTopoTileSource` when `!tile` — potential infinite no-op).
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] - `nitromarkup.js` `markUp` never closes `<pre>` if input ends while `incode` is true.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] - `nitromarkup.js` `markTags` loop `for(var i=0;i<bits.length-1;i+=2)` drops the trailing segment
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] after the last pair.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] What looks genuinely new/interesting
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] - The NZTM↔WGS grid interpolation using precomputed `meters`/`degrees` rows is a nice trick for
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] cheap runtime coordinate conversion.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] - `Bits` + `digTopo` for basement excavation during roof tracing is a neat way to punch holes in
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] terrain that follow building outlines.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] - The worker-vsynth architecture — worklet holding `VSynth`, main thread holding a thin
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] `VSynthNode` — is clean separation, and the `pcm64` hex-string→Float64 path is unusual but
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] workable.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] - The `Surface.div()` subdivision with a `divs` map keyed by vertex-pair hex is a nice way to avoid
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] duplicate midpoints during icosphere refinement.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] If you want, I can go through one file at a time with specific patches — `worker-vsynth.js` and
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] `wire_topo.js` look like the highest-value places to start.
2026-09-25 22:11:38 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:25 [skid@archlinux] how can i enable the digtopo code
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.011 426.2KB 18.99s
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] The `digTopo` path exists but is disabled at two points, and there's an ordering problem you'll hit
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] once you turn it on. Here's the full chain.
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 1. The feature switches
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] In `wire_graphics.js`, `Builder.buildTile` starts with a block of `const` flags. `digOutline` is
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] called from inside the `build_roof2` block:
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] const build_roof=false;
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] const build_roof2=false;         // <-- gate on the whole roof block
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] const build_roof_basement=true;  // <-- gate on digOutline inside it
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] Flip `build_roof2` to `true`. `build_roof_basement` is already `true`, so that's the only flag you
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] need. `build_roof` and `build_roof_geom`/`build_walls_geom` control separate features (scratch
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] drawing, wall geometry) — leave them off initially.
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 2. The ordering problem
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] `meshTile` in `wire_graphics.js` does this, in order:
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] tile.createScratch(AppPlatform.ScratchRes);
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] const surfaces = this.builder.buildTile(tile, 2);   // digOutline runs here
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] ...
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] const topo = tile.topoSurface;                       // already built!
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] tile.tilemesh = layer0.addTileShape(topo, ...);
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] But `tile.topoSurface` was created earlier — either in `TopoTile.onBump` (worker path) or in
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] `GraphicsStack.bakeTileSurface` (no-baker path) — before `digOutline` mutated
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] `heightmap.ground`/`heightmap.diff`. So the dig will silently do nothing visible.
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] You need to rebuild the surface after `buildTile` returns. Add this to `meshTile`:
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] meshTile(tile, home){
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 	this._activeTiles.push(tile);
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 	const tx = (tile.x)*TileSize - home[0];
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 	const ty = (tile.y)*TileSize - home[1];
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 	const origin = [tx, ty, 0x4000];
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 	const layer0 = this.view0.layer;
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 	tile.createScratch(AppPlatform.ScratchRes);
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 	const surfaces = this.builder.buildTile(tile, 2);
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:44 [deepseek-flash] 	// digTopo may have mutated heightmap.ground/diff; rebuild the surface
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 	if(tile.heightmap){
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 		const topo = CreateTopoSurface(128, 16, GroundPen, tile.heightmap);
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 		const zfactor = 4.6/(tile.level*8.0);
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 		topo.calcNormals(zfactor);
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 		tile.topoSurface = topo;
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 	}
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 	tile.allocWallmap();
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 	tile.staticmeesh = layer0.addStaticShapes(surfaces, tile.scratch, tile.wallmap, origin);
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 	const topo = tile.topoSurface;
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 	tile.tilemesh = layer0.addTileShape(topo, tile.basemap, tile.scratch, origin);
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] `GroundPen` is declared in `wire_topo.js`, and the `zfactor` expression matches what
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] `TopoTile.onBump` uses — keep them consistent or the shading will differ between dug and undug
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] tiles.
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 3. Prerequisites
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] - `AppRuntime.UseLINZ` must be `true` (it is by default in `wire_grid.js`).
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] - The tile source must actually have a `roa` layer with `ROOF` outlines. That means the source
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] entry needs `"roaname" in layer` and `level < 3` in `fetchTopoTile`. The `ROOF` array in
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] `onDrawingResponse` will otherwise be empty and the whole `build_roof2` block is skipped by
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] `roofs.length`.
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] - `BasementDepth` in `wire_grid.js` is currently `4`. That's tiny — the dig will be nearly
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] invisible. Bump it to something like `200` to test.
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 4. Debugging the bits
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] `Bits.digTopo` depends on `Bits.poly` producing a consistent even-odd scanline, and `poly` has two
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] rough edges that will bite on complex roofs:
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] - it only clamps `x`, not `y` (negative `y` silently drops writes on the typed array);
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] - it steps one pixel per Bresenham increment, so a thin diagonal can leave single-pixel gaps, and
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] `digTopo`'s `on = !on` toggling then inverts the wrong region.
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] Before wiring it up, call `bits.dump()` on a known roof outline to see the filled region as `X`/`.`
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] ASCII. If the fill looks correct in the dump, `digTopo` will behave. If it looks like a stipple
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] pattern instead of a solid interior, the `poly` fill is the thing to fix, not `digTopo`.
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 5. What `digTopo` actually writes
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] For reference, so you can verify the effect is reaching the surface:
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] heightmap.setGround(tx+bx, ty+by, depth);  // pickGround returns this when nonzero
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] heightmap.setHeight(tx+bx, ty+by, 0);      // clears diff (detail height)
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] `CreateTopoSurface` reads `pickTopo(...).ground` and uses `const z = ground || minz;` — so a
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] nonzero `ground` overrides the terrain elevation and the vertex drops to the basement depth. The
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] `border` argument (40 in the call site) is a halo in heightmap pixels, not world units —
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] worth tuning once you can see it working.
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] Start with `BasementDepth = 200`, `build_roof2 = true`, and the `meshTile` rebuild in place. If
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] nothing appears, dump `bits` first.
2026-09-25 22:12:45 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:04 [skid@archlinux] if i want default height map to include lidar height is there already a setting
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.010 430.9KB 17.59s
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] Short answer: no, there's no runtime setting that switches on LiDAR. The topo grid choice is
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] data-driven from `json/tiles.json`, and the code will fetch whatever `toponame` each layer entry
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] names.
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] Where the choice actually lives
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] `TopoTile.fetchTopoTile` in `wire_topo.js` builds the URL from the layer's `toponame`:
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] if("toponame" in layer){
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 	const src="topogrid/topo-"+layer.toponame+"/"+level+"/"+x+"/"+y+".raw";
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 	...
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 	this.fetchTileRaw(src2);
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 	notopo=false;
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] So whether you get LiDAR-derived tiles, contour-derived DEM, or something else is decided entirely
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] by what `tiles.json` says. There's no mip variant for topo either — unlike aerial, which picks
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] `TopoMipName[res]` / `AppPlatform.MipName` (`""` vs `"sd-"`). The `.raw` path is fixed.
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] The only two flags in `AppRuntime` (`wire_grid.js`) are:
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] let AppRuntime={
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 	UseAerial:true,
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 	UseLINZ:true
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] };
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] `UseAerial` gates the JPEG overlay, `UseLINZ` gates the `roagrid` drawing JSON (roads, roofs,
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] coast, etc.). Neither touches the topo `.raw`.
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] The combining mechanism already exists
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] `TopoSource.listSources` returns every layer in `this.visible` that matches `level` and bounds. If
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] `tiles.json` lists two topo layers for the same tile, both `.raw` files are fetched and handed to
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] the worker, which merges them via `orBytes`:
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] function createTopo(){
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 	const bytes=heightmaps[0]||[];
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 	const heightmap=AllocBumpmap(bytes);
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 	heightmap.setBytes(bytes);
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 	const n=heightmaps.length;
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 	for(let i=1;i<n;i++){
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 		if(heightmaps[i].length==bytes.length){
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 			heightmap.orBytes(heightmaps[i]);
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 		}
2026-09-25 22:16:22 [deepseek-flash] 		...
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 	}
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] Two important caveats about that path:
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] - `orBytes` is overwrite where nonzero, not max or blend. It only writes where the incoming
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] source has `h != 0`. So order in `sourceList` decides who wins — and that order is just the order
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] of `tiles.aerial.concat(tiles.topo, tiles.roa)` in the `TopoSource` constructor. If you add a LiDAR
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] layer alongside a base DEM, whichever is later in that concatenation overwrites the other on
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] overlapping pixels.
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] - It requires the two heightmaps to have the same byte length (`heightmaps[i].length ==
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] bytes.length`). Different resolutions won't merge — the else branch is commented out.
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] So "include LiDAR" via the existing path means: put a LiDAR-derived topo entry in `tiles.json` at
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] the right `layer` and with the right `bounds`, either replacing the existing topo entry or adding
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] it after it.
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] If you want an explicit switch
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] Adding one is straightforward and mirrors `UseAerial`. In `wire_grid.js`:
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] let AppRuntime={
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 	UseAerial:true,
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 	UseLINZ:true,
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 	UseLiDAR:false
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] };
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] Then in `fetchTopoTile`, gate the LiDAR-named layer:
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] if("toponame" in layer){
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 	const useLidar = layer.toponame.startsWith("lidar");
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 	if(!useLidar || AppRuntime.UseLiDAR){
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 		const src="topogrid/topo-"+layer.toponame+"/"+level+"/"+x+"/"+y+".raw";
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 		...
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 		this.fetchTileRaw(src2);
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 	}
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] That assumes your `tiles.json` naming convention identifies LiDAR entries — check what the
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] `toponame` values actually are before committing to a predicate. If they're opaque (`"ak17"`,
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] `"nz1"`), you'll need a per-entry boolean like `layer.lidar: true` instead.
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] One thing that will bite you
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] `heal()` is commented out in `worker_baker.js`:
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] // simon come here and fix up flora
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] //	heightmap.heal(n);
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] LiDAR coverage is patchy — swaths, water masks, no-data areas all come back as `h == 0`. The
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] worker's `setBytes` converts those to `topo[i] = -TopoLevel`, which is a full 16384 units below the
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] topo origin. On screen that's a deep pit, not a flat gap. If you enable LiDAR and see scattered
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] depressions, that's this. `heal()` was meant to fill them (the body is a stub — it counts zeros but
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] doesn't actually repair), so you'd be implementing it, not just uncommenting it.
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] The main-thread `Bumpmap.setBytes` in `wire_topo.js` doesn't have the `else` branch, so the
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] no-baker path leaves zeros as `h = 0` (interpreting as sea-level topo offset). Different symptom,
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] same root cause.
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] What to check first
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] Open `json/tiles.json` and look at the `topo` array entries — specifically the `toponame` fields
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] and the `layer` numbers. That will tell you whether LiDAR is already in the default set, whether
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] it's a separate entry you can toggle, or whether you need to add one. Without that file I can't
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] tell you which.
2026-09-25 22:16:23 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:30 [skid@archlinux] why is flora layer so dark?
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.015 557.2KB 27.33s
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] Two separate problems combine here, and the primary one is not in the palette.
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] Primary cause: flora has zero normals
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] The flora surface is posted by `worker_baker.js` with normals explicitly zeroed:
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] v.push([mx,my,mz,c, 0,0,0,0]);
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] //                 ^^^^^^^^^ nx,ny,nz all zero
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] Compare with `CreateTopoSurface` in the same worker — also zero normals, but it gets fixed up
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] afterwards in `TopoTile.onBump`:
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] const surface=CreateTopoSurface(128,16,GroundPen,bump);
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] const zfactor=4.6/(this.level*8.0);
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] surface.calcNormals(zfactor);   // <-- fixes the tile surface
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] this.topoSurface=surface;
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] Flora has no equivalent. `onFlora` just stores it:
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] onFlora(verts,indices){
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash]     this.floraSurface=new Surface(verts,indices,[]);
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] And `addTileShape` does not compute normals the way `addFlatShape` does:
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] addFlatShape(surface,origin,count){
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash]     shape.addFlatSurface(surface);   // -> flatifyVerts -> calcNormals
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash]     ...
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] addTileShape(surface,basemap,scratch,origin){
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash]     ...
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash]     shape.addSurface(surface);       // raw verts32, no normal pass
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash]     ...
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] The tile shader has `sun` as a uniform and `normal` as an attribute, so lighting is `dot(normal,
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] sun)`. With normals all zero, that's zero — you get ambient only, which reads as a very dark flat
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] surface. That's the symptom.
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] Fix: compute normals for the flora surface, using the same `zfactor` expression as `onBump` so
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] shading is consistent with the terrain underneath.
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] onFlora(verts,indices){
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash]     this.floraSurface=new Surface(verts,indices,[]);
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash]     const zfactor=4.6/(this.level*8.0);
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash]     this.floraSurface.calcNormals(zfactor);
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] If you'd rather keep the main thread free, call `calcNormals` in the worker's `postFlora` before
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] posting — but `calcNormals` uses the `Surface` class, and `worker_baker.js` doesn't import
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] `wire_surface.js`, so you'd have to duplicate the routine. Doing it in `onFlora` is the smaller
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] change.
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] Secondary: `FloraPen=32` is out of palette range
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] `RoaPalette` has 30 entries (indices 0–29). `FloraPen` is defined as `32` in `worker_baker.js`:
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] const FloraPen=32;
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] WebGL uniform-array out-of-bounds reads are undefined; in practice most drivers return 0, so the
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] flora colour term collapses to black on top of the normal problem. If your intended colour was a
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] green, use one of the palette indices that actually is green — 4 (`G,B,G,A`), 15 (`G,B,G,A` with
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] alpha), or 24 (`0,G,0,A`). Given the terrain uses `GroundPen=1` and the tree-like flora sits on
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] grass, `15` is a reasonable pick.
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] If you change `FloraPen`, do it in one place and pass it through to `postFlora`; right now it's a
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] hard-coded constant on the worker side.
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] Why it looks "dark" rather than "missing"
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] The flora geometry is still emitted — that's why you see a shape — but with NdotL at zero and a
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] black palette entry, it's only the alpha-blended fringe against the terrain below that keeps it
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] visible at all. Once you add the `calcNormals` call, you should see it light up immediately; fixing
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] the palette index after that will give it the colour you actually intended.
2026-09-27 22:58:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:25:07 [skid@archlinux] have set pen to 31, how do i change from what is now transparent green
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.011 560.7KB 13.54s
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 31 is still out of range — `RoaPalette` in `wire_core.js` has 30 entries, indices 0–29. Whatever
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] colour you're seeing is coming from the driver's undefined-behaviour read of the `palette` uniform,
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] which is why it's not what you'd expect.
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] How the colour actually gets picked
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] The vertex `xyzc` attribute carries a colour byte in `c`. In `worker_baker.js`, `postFlora` writes
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] that byte from the `c` argument, which is `FloraPen`. The tile fragment shader uses it to index a
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] `palette` uniform:
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] this.RawPalette=new Float32Array(RoaPalette)   // wire_core.js
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] layer.setFloats("palette",gpu.RawPalette);     // wire_graphics.js
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] Each palette entry is 4 floats (r,g,b,a). So both the colour and the transparency come from
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] the same index — the 4th float is alpha.
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] The green entries, with their alpha
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] Reading `RoaPalette`, the entries that read as green are:
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] ╭─────────┬──────────────────────┬─────────┬────────────────────────────────╮
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] │  Index  │  RGB                 │  Alpha  │  Look                          │
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] ├─────────┼──────────────────────┼─────────┼────────────────────────────────┤
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] │  4      │  (0.40, 0.70, 0.40)  │  0.94   │  muted green, **opaque**       │
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] │  9      │  (0.40, 0.94, 0.70)  │  0.94   │  mint, **opaque**              │
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] │  15     │  (0.40, 0.70, 0.40)  │  0.52   │  muted green, **transparent**  │
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] │  20     │  (0.40, 0.94, 0.70)  │  0.52   │  mint, **transparent**         │
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] │  24     │  (0, 0.40, 0)        │  0.52   │  dark green, **transparent**   │
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] │  25     │  (0, 0.40, 0.70)     │  0.52   │  teal, **transparent**         │
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] ╰─────────┴──────────────────────┴─────────┴────────────────────────────────╯
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] The `O` constant is `0.94` (opaque-ish), the `A` constant is `0.52` (transparent). So the
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] transparent green you're seeing is one of the `A`-alpha entries — most likely 15 or 24.
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] If you want an opaque green, set `FloraPen` to `4` or `9`.
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] If none of those is the green you want
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] Add a new entry to `RoaPalette` in `wire_core.js`:
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] const RoaPalette=[
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 	...
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 	G,G,G,A,      // 29
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 	0.15,0.55,0.15,0.94   // 30 - new opaque forest green
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] ];
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] Then set `FloraPen=30` in `worker_baker.js`.
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] Two caveats:
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] - The tile shader declares `uniform vec4 palette[N]`. If `N` is currently 30, the 31st entry will
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] be dropped or the program will fail to link depending on the driver. Check the shader source and
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] bump `N` if you're adding beyond index 29.
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] - `RawPalette` is `new Float32Array(RoaPalette)`, so it picks up the new length automatically — no
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] change needed there.
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] Also still worth doing
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] The transparent green will still look wrong under lighting until you add the `calcNormals` call in
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] `TopoTile.onFlora` — the flora verts ship with `0,0,0` normals, so NdotL is zero and the shader
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] falls back to ambient. Fix that first, then the palette change will read as intended rather than as
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] a flat dark silhouette.
2026-09-27 23:25:21 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:35 [skid@archlinux] is there code for removing building shape from flora layer,
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.013 563.8KB 20.21s
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] No — nothing in the codebase removes building footprints from the flora surface. The pieces needed
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] are all present, but they're not wired together.
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] Why buildings currently show up in flora
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] Flora is generated in the worker, from the merged heightmap, before any drawing data reaches the
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] tile:
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] // worker_baker.js createTopo()
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] heightmap.postFlora(256,8,FloraPen,22);   // <-- flora built here
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] heightmap.post();
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] The mask inside `postFlora` is purely `diff`-based:
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] const m=this.pickHeightMinMax(x*p,y*p);
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] if(m.min>floraMin){        // floraMin = 22
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 	const h1=m.max;
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 	a[y][x]=h0+h1;
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] `pickHeight` reads `this.diff`, and on LiDAR that second channel is high around building edges, so
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] those cells pass the `>22` test. There is no reference to roofs, ground, or any exclusion mask
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] anywhere in the worker.
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] What already exists that does the right thing
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] `TopoTile.digOutline` (in `wire_topo.js`) is invoked per roof outline when `build_roof2` is on. It
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] calls `Bits.digTopo`, which does exactly the zeroing you'd want:
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] // wire_util.js
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] heightmap.setGround(tx+bx, ty+by, depth);
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] heightmap.setHeight(tx+bx, ty+by, 0);   // <-- diff becomes 0
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] `setHeight` writes to `diff`, so any cell inside the footprint (plus a `border` halo) fails the
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] `m.min > floraMin` test. If `TopoFloraSurface` ran after `digTopo`, buildings would already
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] be excluded — no new exclusion logic needed.
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] And `TopoFloraSurface` does exist, in `wire_core.js`, marked "moved to baker" but never deleted:
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] // moved to baker
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] function TopoFloraSurface(count,mag,c,topo,floraMin){
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 	...
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] It is byte-for-byte the same algorithm as the worker's `postFlora`, minus the
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] `postMessage`/transfer at the end.
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] The ordering problem
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] The worker runs at fetch time. `digOutline` runs at mesh time, much later, and only inside
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] `Builder.buildTile`:
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] meshTile(tile,home){
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 	...
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 	const surfaces=this.builder.buildTile(tile,2);   // digOutline fires here
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 	...
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 	const floraSurface=tile.floraSurface;            // <-- already built by worker
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 	const floramesh=layer0.addTileShape(floraSurface,...);
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] So even with `build_roof2` enabled, the flora surface was constructed before the dig and is
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] unaffected. Regenerating it after `buildTile` is the whole fix.
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] Minimal patch
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] In `wire_graphics.js` `meshTile`, after `buildTile` returns and before the flora mesh is uploaded,
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] rebuild the flora surface on the main thread:
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] const surfaces=this.builder.buildTile(tile,2);
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] tile.allocWallmap();
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] tile.staticmeesh=layer0.addStaticShapes(surfaces,tile.scratch,tile.wallmap,origin);
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] const topo=tile.topoSurface;
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] tile.tilemesh=layer0.addTileShape(topo,tile.basemap,tile.scratch,origin);
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] // flora is rebuilt here so that digOutline has already zeroed diff
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] // over building footprints
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] const floraSurface=TopoFloraSurface(256,8,FloraPen,tile.heightmap,22);
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] const zfactor=4.6/(tile.level*8.0);
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] floraSurface.calcNormals(zfactor);
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] tile.floraSurface=floraSurface;
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] const floramesh=layer0.addTileShape(floraSurface,tile.basemap,tile.scratch,origin);
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] tile.floramesh=floramesh;
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] `FloraPen` is defined in `wire_topo.js`, which is loaded alongside `wire_graphics.js`, so it's in
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] scope.
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] Caveats
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] - `build_roof2` must be on. It's `false` by default in `Builder.buildTile`, so `digOutline`
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] never runs. Flip it to `true` and you'll get the roof-block behaviour — including the basement
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] fill, the roof wall pixel accounting, and the `context.clip()` region, all of which will now
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] execute. If you only want the dig and not the rest, the cleaner change is to split the block: call
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] `tile.digOutline(outline,BasementDepth,40)` in a small loop before the `build_roof2` block, leaving
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] `build_roof2` off.
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] - The worker still generates its flora. With this patch, `tile.floraSurface` is overwritten
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] on the main thread, so the worker's version is discarded — but it was still computed and
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] transferred, which is wasted work. To reclaim it, delete the `heightmap.postFlora(...)` call in
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] `worker_baker.js createTopo()` and drop the `flora` case from `TileInstance.onMessage`. Otherwise
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] you're paying for two flora passes per tile.
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] - `border=40` is generous. `digOutline` takes a `border` in heightmap pixels, so each
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] footprint gets a 40-pixel halo of zeroed `diff`. At LiDAR resolution that's several metres —
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] probably fine for tree exclusion, but tune it once you can see the result.
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] - Normals. The worker's `postFlora` ships `0,0,0` normals, and `TopoFloraSurface` inherits
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] that (`v.push([mx,my,mz,c, 0,0,0,0])`). Calling `calcNormals` after rebuilding is what lights the
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] surface — otherwise the flora will still look dark, independent of any exclusion.
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] - `AppRuntime.UseLINZ` and `level<3` gate whether roof drawings are fetched at all in
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] `fetchTopoTile`. Both are already `true`/`<3` in the default config, but worth confirming for
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] whichever level you're viewing.
2026-09-27 23:27:56 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:30 [skid@archlinux] cant i post once footprints are dug?
2026-09-27 23:29:49 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.011 569.0KB 17.21s
2026-09-27 23:29:49 [deepseek-flash] Yes — and it's the cleaner shape. But it means moving the dig into the worker, because the worker
2026-09-27 23:29:49 [deepseek-flash] can't do it after the fact.
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] Why the worker can't do it post-hoc
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] `Bumpmap.post()` transfers the buffers away:
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] const transfer=[topo.buffer,diff.buffer,ground.buffer];
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] postMessage(message,transfer);
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] After that the worker's `topo`/`diff`/`ground` are detached. Even if you sent a `dig` command
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] later, there's nothing to dig into. So "post once footprints are dug" only works if the dig happens
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] in the worker, before the transfer.
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] What that requires
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] Three things move to the worker side:
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] - `Bits` from `wire_util.js` — the polygon rasteriser. Currently only loaded on the main
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] thread.
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] - `TopoTile.digOutline` — or a shared version of it. It's ~30 lines and reads only from the
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] heightmap and the outline.
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] - A protocol change — the worker must not `post()` until it has received the roof outlines.
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] And one thing has to be reordered on the main thread: `onDrawingResponse` must forward the roof
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] outlines to the worker, and `createTopo()` in the worker must defer its `postFlora` + `post` until
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] those arrive.
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] New flow
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] main                          worker
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] ────                          ──────
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] fetchTileRaw  ──heightmap──▶  accumulate
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] fetchDrawing                  (hold, don't post yet)
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash]   onDrawingResponse
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash]     extract ROOF outlines
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash]                 ──dig───────▶  apply dig to topo/diff/ground
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash]                                postFlora(256,8,FloraPen,22)
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash]                                post({bump, flora})  ──▶ main
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash]                               (transfer buffers now)
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] Because the dig runs first, both the bump surface and the flora surface are built from the same dug
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] state. The main thread's `onBump` creates `topoSurface` from an already-dug heightmap — no rebuild
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] in `meshTile` needed. The whole "rebuild after buildTile" workaround disappears.
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] The two messages become one
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] Right now `postFlora` and `post` each fire their own `postMessage`. Collapse them:
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] function postAll(){
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 	const message=[{
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 		command:"bump",
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 		topo:this.topo, diff:this.diff, ground:this.ground,
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 		floraVerts:floraVerts, floraIndices:floraIndices
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 	}];
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 	const transfer=[
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 		this.topo.buffer, this.diff.buffer, this.ground.buffer,
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 		floraVerts.buffer, floraIndices.buffer
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 	];
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 	postMessage(message,transfer);
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] Then `TileInstance.onMessage` handles one case instead of two, and `hasLoaded()` loses the
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] `topoCount == heightmaps.length` / `floraSurface` conditions — the tile is loaded the moment the
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] single message arrives.
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] The gate on the main thread
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] The worker only knows when to build once both the topo and the drawing have landed.
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] `TopoTile.hasLoaded()` already tracks both (`topoCount == heightmaps.length` and `drawingCount ==
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] drawings.length`), but the worker's `refresh()` currently fires on the topo count alone. So the
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] worker needs to know to wait.
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] Two ways:
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] - Send a `dig` command from the main thread once `hasLoaded()` is true, carrying the
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] extracted roof outlines. Worker applies the dig, then builds. Simple, one-shot, no extra state.
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] - Send `topoCount` and `drawingCount` to the worker and let it decide. More coupling, not
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] worth it.
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] The first is cleaner. `TopoGrid.refreshTiles` / `populateHome` already gate on `hasLoaded()`, so
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] you have a natural place to fire the dig message.
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] Gotchas
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] - `BasementDepth` is 4. That's a near-invisible dig. The outline points are halved before
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] hitting `Bits` (`o[0]/2`), and `digTopo` writes `depth` directly into `ground`. At `4` the surface
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] barely moves. Bump it to something like `200` before you can tell it's working.
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] - `heightmaps.length=0` in `createTopo`. If you defer the build, don't clear until after
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] the dig. The merged `heightmap` object survives, but the raw `heightmaps` array is what the count
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] is compared against.
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] - `fetchTileRaw` bumps `topoCount` on the main thread, and that count is what the worker is
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] told via `bakeTopo(count)`. If drawings arrive first (common — they're small JSON, topo is a big
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] binary), the dig message can arrive before `topoCount` is satisfied. Either buffer the dig in the
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] worker until the topo count is met, or gate on the main thread so the dig is only sent once
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] `hasLoaded()` is true. The latter is simpler.
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] - `level<3` gate. `fetchTopoTile` only fetches `roaname` drawings when `level<3`. So at
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] level 3+ there are no roof outlines and the dig is a no-op. That's fine — the flora just comes out
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] un-dug, same as today.
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] - `Bits.poly` clamps `x` but not `y`. A negative `y` writes off the end of the typed array
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] silently, and `digTopo` then toggles the wrong region. Roof outlines near the tile edge will hit
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] this. Fix the clamp before relying on the dig.
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] - Duplicate `Bits` and `digOutline`. If you don't want to maintain two copies, load
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] `wire_util.js` into the worker via `importScripts`, and factor `digOutline` into a free function
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] that both the worker and `TopoTile` call. That's the tidier option, at the cost of a small
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] refactor.
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] Is it worth it
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] Compared to rebuilding flora on the main thread after `buildTile`:
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] ╭──────────────────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────╮
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] │                                                  │  Main-thread rebuild  │  Worker dig                   │
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] │  Flora passes per tile                           │  2                    │  1                            │
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] │  Code churn                                      │  ~6 lines             │  ~120 lines, protocol change  │
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] │  Ordering guarantees                             │  implicit             │  explicit                     │
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] │  Fits existing "everything in the worker" shape  │  no                   │  yes                          │
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] ╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────╯
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] If you're already planning to touch the worker protocol, the worker dig is the right end state. If
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] you just want to see it working, the rebuild is a five-minute change and you can migrate later.
2026-09-27 23:29:50 [deepseek-flash] 
