2026-09-29 19:57:38 [skid@archlinux] is there a ztest in action?
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.023 1.553MB 18.72s
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] Yes — but only for part of the frame, and that matters for how you read the gap.
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] Where it's turned on
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] `drawLayerList` in `wire_graphics.js` walks two passes:
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] for(const pass of ["depthDisable","depthEnable"]){
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 	if(pass=="depthEnable"){
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 		draw.push({command:gl.enable,arguments:[gl.DEPTH_TEST]});
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 	}
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 	...draw tagged layers...
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 	if(pass=="depthEnable"){
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 		draw.push({command:gl.disable,arguments:[gl.DEPTH_TEST]});
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 	}
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] So depth test is enabled for the second pass only and disabled at the end of it. It's not global
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] state — it's a raw GL call baked into the draw list.
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] Which layers carry which tag (`GraphicsLayer.reset`):
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] depthDisable only : sprite, line
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] depthEnable only  : model, static, tile, flat, edge
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] both              : point
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] So all the geometry you care about — walls (`static`), terrain (`tile`), roof surfaces (`static`),
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] models — is in the depthEnable pass and z-tests against each other.
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] What that means for your gap
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] Buildings and terrain are z-tested against each other. So the gap you're seeing is real
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] geometry separation — different world z — not a depth-buffer artifact. Depth test can't hide a 100
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] m wall sitting above the ground; it only decides which of two fragments at the same pixel wins.
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] Three things worth knowing while debugging this
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] - Depth func is default `LESS`. Never set anywhere. So equal z fails, and coincident
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] surfaces resolve by draw order.
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] - Blend is globally on. `WebGL2Context.clear` does `gl.enable(gl.BLEND)` and never disables
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] it. Terrain alpha is `0.94`, so it blends. Combined with `LESS`, a blended surface drawn later
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] paints over one at equal depth.
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] - Draw order is static → tile. `rawLayers()` returns `[model, static, tile, flat, line,
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] point, edge]`, and the pass loop preserves that order within each pass. So walls are drawn
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] before terrain. If terrain is in front, it blends 94% over the wall; if behind, depth test
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] rejects it. Neither produces a gap — but it does affect how the seam reads.
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] `depthMask` is left at default (true), so blended surfaces write depth. That's why draw order is
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] load-bearing here.
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] No polygon offset
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] No `gl.polygonOffset`, no depth bias, no `gl.depthFunc` call anywhere. So there's no GL trickery
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] either hiding or creating the gap. What you see on screen is what's in the buffers.
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] If you want to confirm the geometry mismatch is the cause rather than the renderer, the fastest
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] check is to temporarily set `FloraPen`-style wall top to `h0` (zero height) — flat ribbons on the
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] terrain. If those sit flush, the base sampling is right and the problem is elsewhere. If they still
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] float or sink, it's the `pickElevation` vs `pickTopo().min` mismatch from the last answer.
2026-09-29 19:57:57 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:24:32 [skid@archlinux] i want to draw the tiles from back to front, is there any attempt at this currently? we may have discussed
2026-09-29 20:24:34 [skid@archlinux] it earlier
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.024 1.556MB 24.60s
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] No — nothing sorts. Order is whatever the shapes happen to be in.
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] What determines it now
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] `drawLayerList` iterates `layer.shapes` in array order, and `addShape` just pushes. So tile order
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] in the `tile` layer = mesh order = the order `populateHome` first saw each tile `hasLoaded()`.
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] That order is: within one frame, `refreshTiles` scan order (`a` outer, `b` inner); across frames,
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] load-completion order. Fetch priority is `1-(aa+bb)` sorted ascending then popped from the
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] end, so the nearest tiles fetch first and mesh first. Net: roughly nearest-first, i.e.
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] front-to-back. Not deliberate, just a consequence.
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] We did discuss this — the conclusion was that the sort has to live in the draw path, not
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] `meshTile`, because a far tile that loads late still appends after the near ones. Sorting at mesh
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] time can't hold.
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] Where to put it
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] `animate` calls `this.graphics.compilePanels()` every frame, which walks down to
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] `GraphicsLayer.compileLayers` → `drawLayerList`. The draw list is already rebuilt each frame, so a
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] sort there costs nothing structural.
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] drawLayerList(layers){
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 	const gl=this.glcontext.gl;
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 	const draw=[];
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 	const m=this.RawView;
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 	for(const pass of ["depthDisable","depthEnable"]){
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 		...
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 		for(const layer of layers){
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 			if(layer.tags.includes(pass)){
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 				draw.push({command:gl.useProgram,arguments:[layer.program.name]});
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 				const handlesUniform=layer.uniforms["handles"];
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 				const shapes=sortShapes(layer.shapes,m);
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 				for(const shape of shapes){
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 					shape.addDraw(draw,handlesUniform);
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 				}
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 			}
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 		}
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 	}
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 	return draw;
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] function sortShapes(shapes,m){
2026-09-29 20:24:58 [deepseek-flash] 	const a=m[4];
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] 	const b=m[5];
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] 	const keyed=shapes.map((shape,index)=>{
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] 		const t=shape.transforms[0];
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] 		return {shape,depth:a*t[3]+b*t[7],index};
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] 	});
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] 	keyed.sort((p,q)=>p.depth-q.depth);
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] 	return keyed.map(k=>k.shape);
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] `t[3],t[7]` are the shape origin set by `setOrigin`. `m[4],m[5]` are the view matrix's xy→screen-y
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] terms, so `ax+by` is proportional to screen y, and NDC +y is up-screen = back. Ascending
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] sort draws back first.
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] Caveat worth knowing before you commit
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] In this projection `z_clip` is pure world z — row 2 of the matrix is `[0, 0, -1/2048,
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] 0.5]`. So the depth buffer already resolves height ordering correctly and independently of draw
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] order. Draw order only affects things depth can't separate: coplanar seams where the two
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] surfaces are at the same z and `LESS` fails.
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] So if your symptom is "a far tile's terrain paints over a near one", sorting won't fix it — that's
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] depth or winding, not order. If the symptom is seams or flora blending, sorting is right.
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] Two things to check alongside
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] - `flushTile` still never splices `_activeTiles`. It grows forever, so any count you derive from it
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] is wrong.
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] - If you sort, sort only the blended layers. `static` and `tile` are depth-tested and
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] mostly opaque; reordering them changes nothing except cost.
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] Want me to write it as a patch against `wire_graphics.js`? I'd want to see `tile_fragment.gles`
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] first to confirm whether the tile layer is genuinely blended — if terrain alpha is 1.0 there, this
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] whole thing is moot.
2026-09-29 20:24:59 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:27:14 [skid@archlinux] tiles do disapear, maybe we should start with a fix for that if you think
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.033 1.560MB 49.12s
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] Probably. Given the code, the most likely cause is that `flushTile` does a full retire — it
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] throws away the heightmap, the surface, the drawings — so a tile that leaves the 5-tile radius and
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] comes back has to re-fetch from the network and rebuild before it can draw. During that window it's
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] invisible. Pan back and forth and tiles blink.
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] Verify first
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] Two logs, five minutes:
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] // wire_graphics.js flushTile
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] console.log("[flush]",tile.name,tile.frame,this.frameCount);
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] // wire_graphics.js meshTile
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] console.log("[mesh]",tile.name);
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] If you see `[flush]` for a tile that's still on screen, or `[mesh]` for a tile you just saw flush,
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] that's the cycle.
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] The fix: unmesh, don't retire
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] Separate GPU resources from CPU data. On sweep, drop the meshes and GL buffers, keep the heightmap
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] and surfaces. On return, `populateHome` sees `!tile.tilemesh && tile.hasLoaded()` and re-meshes
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] locally — no fetch, no worker.
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] // wire_graphics.js
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] flushTile(tile){
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 	const meesh=[tile.tilemesh,tile.floramesh,tile.staticmeesh,tile.flatmeesh].flat();
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 	for(const mesh of meesh){
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 		if(mesh){
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 			this.graphicsLayers[0].removeMesh(mesh);
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 		}
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 	}
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 	const active=this._activeTiles;
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 	const index=active.indexOf(tile);
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 	if(index>=0){
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 		active.splice(index,1);
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 	}
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 	tile.flushMesh();
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 	// retire() removed — CPU data retained for instant re-mesh
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] Three things this depends on:
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] • `flushed` guard has to go.* `flushMesh` early-returns if `flushed` is true. First flush
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] sets it. Re-mesh doesn't clear it. Second flush is a no-op, so the second set of GL buffers leaks.
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] Delete the guard — the body is already idempotent (`if(this.tilemesh)` etc.).
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] // wire_topo.js TopoTile.flushMesh — delete the first three lines
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] flushMesh(){
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 	if(this.hasBaker){
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 		if(this.instance){
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 			this.instance.terminate();
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 		}
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 		this.instance=null;
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 	}
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 	...
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] • `hasLoaded()` must still be true after unmesh.* It is — `ready`, `heightmap`,
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] `floraSurface`, `imageCount`, `drawingCount` are all untouched by `flushMesh`. Good.
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] • Worker termination.* `flushMesh` terminates the instance. That's fine — the bump is
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] already received, and re-mesh needs nothing from the worker. A future level change creates a new
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] instance.
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] Memory
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] This trades a reload for retention. At `res=1024` each tile holds 3 × 1M × 2 = 6 MB, so flying
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] around accumulates fast. The 20-frame sweep becomes the wrong tool — you want two thresholds:
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] - 20 frames → unmesh (GPU only). Cheap to reverse.
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] - ~200 frames → full `retire()`. Frees the 6 MB.
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] // wire_topo.js TopoGrid
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] sweep(){
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 	const unmesh=[];
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 	const retire=[];
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 	const minFrame=this.frameCount-20;
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 	const killFrame=this.frameCount-200;
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 	const grid=this._tileGrid;
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 	for(const y in grid){
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 		const row=grid[y];
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 		for(const x in row){
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 			const tile=row[x];
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 			if(!tile.frame) continue;
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 			if(tile.frame<killFrame){
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 				retire.push(tile);
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 				tile.frame=0;
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 			}else if(tile.frame<minFrame){
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 				unmesh.push(tile);
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 			}
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 		}
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 	}
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 	return {unmesh,retire};
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] And in `animate`:
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] const swept=tiles.sweep();
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] for(const tile of swept.unmesh){
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 	this.viewPanel.flushTile(tile);
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] for(const tile of swept.retire){
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 	this.viewPanel.retireTile(tile);
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] }
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] with `retireTile` being the current `flushTile` plus `tile.retire()`.
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] Separate leak, fix while you're there
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:28:09 [deepseek-flash] `sweep` skips tiles whose `frame` was never stamped — loading tiles, failed tiles, `pick`-created
