2026-09-29 20:35:43 [skid@archlinux] is there any zbias settings or do i need to share shaders?
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.027 1.646MB 32.27s
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] Yes, there's a `zbias` in every vertex shader — and it's `0.0` in all of them. So the bias
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] mechanism exists but is unused. The thing actually controlling depth is the divisor applied
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] after `v.z`, and that differs between layers.
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] The divisors
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] ╭──────────────────────┬────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────╮
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] │  shader              │  depth line        │  NDC z at world z                │
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] ├──────────────────────┼────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────┤
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] │  static_vertex       │  zbias+v.z/2048.0  │  (0.5 - z/2048) / 2048           │
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] │  flat_vertex         │  zbias+v.z/2048.0  │  (0.5 - z/2048) / 2048           │
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] │  model_vertex        │  zbias+v.z/2048.0  │  (0.5 - z/2048) / 2048           │
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] │  tile_vertex         │  zbias+v.z/4096.0  │  (0.5 - z/2048) / 4096           │
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] │  rayVertex           │  zbias+v.z         │  0.5 - z/2048                    │
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] │  sprite_vertex       │  none              │  raw xyzc, no view transform     │
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] ╰──────────────────────┴────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────╯
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] `v.z` here is post-`view`. The view matrix (`TranslateZoomAngleAspect`) already sets row 2 to `[0,
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 0, -1/2048, 0.5]`, so `v.z = 0.5 - z_world/2048` before the shader's own divide.
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] That's the whole problem. Terrain is at half the depth value of walls at the same world z.
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] With `LESS`, smaller wins, so terrain always beats static at any contested pixel.
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] What that does to a wall base
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] At a pixel where the wall base and terrain coincide in world space (same x, y, z), the wall needs
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] `2*z_wall - z_terrain > 1024` to win — i.e. the wall only becomes visible above roughly 512 world
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] units (32 m at 16 units/m). Everything below that is occluded by the terrain. The wall appears to
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] start above its base, and the visible gap depends on terrain z at that pixel, which is why it
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] "varies".
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] Fix
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] Unify the divisor. `/2048` matches the view matrix term, so make `tile_vertex` match the others:
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] float d=zbias+v.z/2048.0;
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] `rayVertex` at `v.z` (no divisor) is a separate case — its NDC z is ~0.5, far behind everything, so
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] lines and points are always occluded when depth is enabled. If that's not intended, it needs a
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] divisor too. The `lineLayer` is `depthDisable` so it doesn't matter there, but `pointLayer` is
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] tagged both and gets drawn twice.
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] Even with matching divisors
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] At a coincident pixel, terrain and wall are at equal NDC z. `LESS` fails on equal, so draw
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] order decides. `rawLayers()` is `[model, static, tile, ...]`, so static is drawn first, tile second
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] — tile wins the tie. Wall base still hidden.
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] Two ways out, both one line:
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] - Nudge the wall forward. The `zbias` variable is already there:
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash]   float zbias=-0.00005;
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash]   NDC units, so a small constant offset. Shifts the whole wall, top included — usually fine since
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] the top is far above terrain anyway.
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] - Or bias the wall base in world space in `walledSpiral`:
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash]   verts.push([px,-py,g0-8,pu,0,0,0,wallc]);
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash]   `-8` units = 0.5 m below the terrain sample. Sinks the base under the surface so the seam is
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] hidden regardless of depth quirks. Cheapest and most robust.
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] On sharing shaders
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] The depth line is four lines of copy-paste across five shaders. You don't need to share the file —
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] just make the constant identical. If you'd rather enforce it, the cleanest place is the view
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] matrix: pre-multiply `array[10]` by the divisor and drop the shader-side divide entirely, so
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] `gl_Position.z = v.z` everywhere. Then there's one number to get right, in one place.
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] But that changes `hfac`-driven z exaggeration at every level, so do it deliberately, not as a
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] drive-by.
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] Not the cause
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] `hfac = 0.77 / (1<<(level*3))` is uniform across all layers, so level scaling isn't the mismatch.
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] Neither is the `z` term in the view matrix — both layers see it. It's purely the shader-side
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] divide.
2026-09-29 20:36:46 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:38:29 [skid@archlinux] fixed! wow thanks a million
2026-09-29 20:38:34 [deepseek-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.4 deepseek-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.020 1.650MB 4.42s
2026-09-29 20:38:34 [deepseek-flash] Glad that landed — the `/4096` vs `/2048` split was a sneaky one to spot from behaviour alone.
2026-09-29 20:38:34 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:38:34 [deepseek-flash] Two things worth remembering while it's fresh:
2026-09-29 20:38:34 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:38:34 [deepseek-flash] - `rayVertex` has no divisor at all, so `pointLayer` (tagged both passes) sits at NDC z ≈ 0.5 in
2026-09-29 20:38:34 [deepseek-flash] the depth-enabled pass. Lines are `depthDisable` so they're fine, but points will be occluded by
2026-09-29 20:38:34 [deepseek-flash] anything in front. If you use them for picking or markers, they'll need the same `/2048`.
2026-09-29 20:38:34 [deepseek-flash] - `zbias` is now your lever if seams come back at grazing angles — one constant per shader, and
2026-09-29 20:38:34 [deepseek-flash] terrain/walls/tiles are all in the same NDC space now, so it'll behave predictably.
2026-09-29 20:38:34 [deepseek-flash] 
2026-09-29 20:38:34 [deepseek-flash] And the earlier `BasementDepth=4` (~25 cm) is still worth a bump now that the dig can actually be
2026-09-29 20:38:34 [deepseek-flash] seen — you'll get much clearer feedback while tuning.
2026-09-29 20:38:34 [deepseek-flash] 
